window.DOT_I18N={"en":{"brand":"Brand","copyLink":"Copy link","copied":"Copied","heroTitle":"Connecting the Dots","heroBody":"From .arpa to .lugano: how Internet domains became identity, infrastructure and trust.","tabOverview":"Overview","tabArchaeology":"Archaeology","tabHow":"How it works","tabCities":"Cities & territories","tabContention":"Contention","tabStrange":"Strange Internet","tabReveal":"Round 2026","tabPress":"Data & sources","tabSources":"Methodology","applicationsRound":"Applications by round","applicationsRoundSub":"The jump in 2012 changed the scale of the namespace.","icann2012":"2012: what ICANN received","icann2012Sub":"These are separate official classifications, not slices of one pie.","overviewCallout":"What we put after the dot says something about the world we imagine.","overviewFoot":"Official ICANN fields remain separate from editorial interpretation. The point is to read the history without turning it into marketing.","archTitle":"A short archaeology of expansion","archSub":"Some milestones are ICANN rounds; others explain how city TLDs became possible.","timeCapsule":"2000 already looks like a time capsule","timeCapsuleSub":"A curated selection from the proof-of-concept round.","string":"String","theme":"Theme","status":"Status","why":"Why it matters","scopeGeneral":"General principles","scopeGeneralSub":"How the DNS, TLD roles and the new-gTLD process work, independently of .lugano.","scopeLugano":".lugano · applied case","scopeLuganoSub":"What those general rules mean for Lugano’s 2026 application.","luganoAppliedTitle":"From the general rule to .lugano","luganoAppliedBody":"The .lugano application follows the same process described above. Until evaluation, contracting and delegation are completed, .lugano remains an application and is not an active TLD.","luganoExampleLabel":"Hypothetical example","luganoExampleBody":"If .lugano is delegated, the same DNS structure could produce addresses such as servizi.lugano or www.servizi.lugano. This illustrates the hierarchy; it is not a live service.","luganoLanguageGuide":"Speaking precisely about .lugano","luganoLanguageGuideSub":"Two distinctions that matter while the application is still in process.","howTitle":"How Internet domains actually work","howSub":"The essential concepts for navigating Internet domains, DNS and Internet governance.","anatomyTitle":"Start with one address","anatomyBody":"In <b>www.example.com</b>, <b>.com</b> is the top-level domain (TLD). “example” is the name registered directly under it. “www” is an optional label added in front of that registered name.","rootLabel":"Root","rootBody":"The invisible top of the DNS hierarchy.","tldLabel":"Top-level domain","tldBody":"The final part: .ch, .com, .org.","secondLabel":"Registered name","secondBody":"The name immediately to the left of the TLD, for example example.com.","hostLabel":"Optional label","hostBody":"An optional label added in front of the registered name. In www.example.com, “www” can identify a specific site or service; it is not part of the TLD.","whoTitle":"Who does what?","whoSub":"The Internet has no single “owner”. It works through distinct roles, contracts and technical coordination.","roleIcann":"ICANN","roleIcannBody":"Coordinates policies and contracts for generic top-level domains through a global multistakeholder model.","roleIana":"IANA / PTI","roleIanaBody":"Maintains the authoritative Root Zone Database and coordinates technical delegation of TLDs into the DNS root.","roleRegistry":"Registry operator","roleRegistryBody":"Runs the rules and authoritative database for one TLD. The registry operator is responsible for operating that namespace within the ICANN contractual framework.","roleRsp":"Registry Service Provider","roleRspBody":"Provides critical technical registry services such as DNS and registration infrastructure for the registry operator.","roleRegistrar":"Registrar","roleRegistrarBody":"The retail/intermediary layer through which domain names are normally registered and maintained.","roleRegistrant":"Registrant","roleRegistrantBody":"The person or organization that registers and uses a domain name under the TLD.","birthTitle":"How a new TLD is born","birthSub":"The 2026 process is not “apply and receive”. It is a staged public process.","step1":"Application","step1b":"Applicant files the application and pays the required fee.","step2":"Reveal","step2b":"ICANN publishes public application data and identifies identical-string contention.","step3":"Input & objections","step3b":"Community input, formal objections and relevant government advice can enter the process.","step4":"Evaluation","step4b":"String, applicant, operational, financial and technical components are evaluated.","step5":"Contention","step5b":"If multiple applicants seek the same or confusingly similar string, the contention must be resolved.","step6":"Contract","step6b":"A successful applicant signs the Base Registry Agreement with ICANN.","step7":"Delegation","step7b":"After onboarding and technical checks, IANA/PTI can place the TLD in the DNS root.","step8":"Launch","step8b":"Only then can the registry move toward actually using and allocating names under the TLD.","controlTitle":"For .lugano: what the registry could govern, and what it would not mean","canTitle":"What the registry could govern","can1":"Eligibility and allocation policies for names under .lugano, within ICANN rules and applicable law.","can2":"Reserved names, launch phases and local governance choices.","can3":"Registration-policy and security requirements for names operating under .lugano.","cannotTitle":"What it would not mean","cannot1":"Lugano does not “own the Internet” or control .ch, .com or other TLDs.","cannot2":"An application is not an assignment: .lugano is not active until the process, contract and delegation are complete.","cannot3":"ICANN does not operate the City’s websites; it coordinates the global naming system and the gTLD contractual framework.","sourceGuide":"Official explainer","cityTitle":"From place name to public infrastructure","citySub":"The city-TLD idea predates the 2012 round by more than a decade.","geoLayer":"2012 geographic layer","geoApps":"applications self-identified as geographic","geoDelegated":"geographic gTLDs delegated from the 2012 round in ICANN statistics","secondGen":"A second generation of city TLDs?","secondGenBody":"The first wave was largely about identity, locality and registration. A 2026 city namespace can also be designed around trusted public services, verifiable identity, machine-readable governance and interaction with AI agents. That is an editorial hypothesis, not an ICANN classification.","contentionTitle":"What everyone wanted in 2012","contentionSub":"Contention is a surprisingly good snapshot of the app, platform and e-commerce era.","strangeTitle":"The strange Internet","strangeSub":"Not just odd names. These are small fossils of what each era thought deserved its own piece of the Internet.","searchPlaceholder":"Search .wtf, mobile, identity, trust…","allRounds":"All rounds","crossRound":"Cross-round","allCategories":"All categories","reset":"Reset","searchHint":"Search a TLD, a social theme or an idea.","results":"results","noResults":"No matches. Even the Internet occasionally runs out of weirdness.","source":"Source","sourcesN":"Sources","whatItWas":"What it was","whatItSays":"What it says about the era","strangeNext":"<b>2026 layer:</b> after Reveal Day, official applications will be classified with the same discipline as the historical material: the application record is a FACT; the cultural or technological meaning we derive from it is a READING.","official2026":"2026 official state","paidApps":"paid applications proceeding","revealBody":"ICANN will publish the public application data on 7 October 2026 at 18:00 UTC, including primary strings and identical-string contention sets.","preRevealIntel":"Pre-Reveal intelligence","disclosed":"publicly disclosed strings tracked by TLDz as of 26 Sep 2026","secondaryNote":"Secondary source. It is not the official ICANN application list and will be superseded by Reveal Day data.","importPlan":"Reveal-Day import plan","pressTitle":"Data, facts & reusable resources","pressSub":"Verified facts, definitions and reusable resources for understanding the data without overstating what it means.","safeFacts":"Five key facts","copyFact":"Copy","languageGuide":"Precise words, clear concepts","languageGuideSub":"The essential concepts for navigating domains, DNS and Internet governance.","sayThis":"More precise","notThis":"Not the same thing","safe1":"The City of Lugano has applied for the new top-level domain .lugano.","avoid1":"Lugano has obtained / owns the .lugano domain.","safe2":"A top-level domain is the final part of an Internet address, such as .ch or .com.","avoid2":"A TLD is simply a new website address.","safe3":"ICANN coordinates the global domain-name system and the new-gTLD process.","avoid3":"ICANN is the government or owner of the Internet.","safe4":"If successful, .lugano could become a governed namespace under rules defined by its registry operator within the ICANN framework.","avoid4":"Anyone will automatically be able to register any .lugano name.","downloadsTitle":"Download the working data","downloadsSub":"Download the underlying datasets for verification, reuse or further analysis.","downloadStrange":"Strange Internet dataset","downloadRounds":"Round summary","downloadContention":"2012 contention","downloadGlossary":"DNS & governance glossary","downloadFacts":"Key facts","downloadPack":"Download full data pack (.zip)","anglesTitle":"Questions raised by the data","angle1":"How the language of TLD applications mirrors the society of each era.","angle2":"From .berlin to .lugano: how cities entered the DNS root.","angle3":"Why “application” and “delegation” are very different moments.","angle4":"What the 2026 contention sets reveal about AI, agents, finance, brands and identity.","angle5":"Can a city TLD evolve from digital branding into trust infrastructure?","sourcesTitle":"Sources & methodology","sourcesSub":"Primary sources are preferred. Secondary sources are explicitly labelled.","guardrails":"Methodological guardrails","primary":"Primary","secondary":"Secondary","footerLeft":"Pre-Reveal prototype · data frozen 1 Oct 2026","footerRight":"Static, local-first and ready for public hosting.","applications":"applications","applicants":"applicants","geographic":"Geographic","community":"Community","idn":"IDN","round2000":"Proof of concept","round2004":"Sponsored TLD round","round2012":"Mass expansion","round2026":"Next round","tl1984Title":"Before .com, there was .arpa","tl1984Body":"RFC 920 described .arpa as the initial, transitional top-level domain for the ARPA-Internet while the DNS was being introduced. It was supposed to disappear; instead it survived and today serves Internet infrastructure.","tl1985Title":"The first .com: symbolics.com","tl1985Body":"In January 1985, .com, .edu, .gov, .mil, .net and .org joined .arpa in the root. On 15 March, symbolics.com became the first registered .com domain.","tl1999Title":"The city idea appears","tl1999Body":"The .berlin concept is traced back to 1999.","tl2000Title":"Proof of concept","tl2000Body":"47 applications. Even .nyc appears, then withdraws.","tl2004Title":"Sponsored communities","tl2004Body":"10 applications for 9 strings: .asia, .cat, .jobs, .mail, .mobi, .post, .tel, .travel and .xxx.","tl2012Title":"The explosion","tl2012Body":"1,930 applications, about 1,400 unique strings and 66 geographic applications.","tl2014Title":"Cities go live","tl2014Body":".berlin opens to the public; .nyc and .paris are delegated.","tl2026Title":"The next layer","tl2026Body":"1,616 paid applications proceed. Reveal Day: 7 October 2026. .lugano enters the picture.","city":"City","children":"Children","health":"Health","labour":"Labour","publicInterest":"Public interest","techFossil":"Tech fossil","geography":"Geography","culture":"Culture","identity":"Identity","publicInfrastructure":"Public infrastructure","oddity":"Oddity","socialMirror":"Social mirror","optimism":"Optimism","trust":"Trust","adult":"Adult","personalBrand":"Personal brand","statusCulture":"Status culture","intimacy":"Intimacy","politics":"Politics","faith":"Faith","lifestyle":"Lifestyle","protest":"Protest","withdrawn":"Withdrawn","returned":"Returned","evaluated":"Evaluated","selected":"Selected","proposed":"Proposed","delegated":"Delegated","notDelegated":"Not delegated","longitudinal":"Proposed → delegated","cNyc":"A city-TLD application already existed in 2000.","cKids":"Several applicants wanted a dedicated namespace for children.","cHealth":"The World Health Organization proposed a dedicated health namespace.","cUnion":"The international trade-union movement applied for .union.","cWap":"A pure fossil from the WAP/mobile-web era.","cGeo":"A geographic namespace concept well before the 2012 city wave.","cMuseum":"One of the seven selected proof-of-concept TLDs.","cName":"Personal identity imagined directly in the namespace.","cPost":"Public-service infrastructure; the postal sector returned with .post in 2004.","sKids":"Multiple applicants proposed .kids in 2000; one application also paired it with .xxx.","iKids":"The early Web was already asking whether children needed a distinct, governed space rather than merely filtered content.","sHealth":"WHO proposed a dedicated .health namespace.","iHealth":"Long before today’s arguments about online misinformation, public-health actors were already thinking about trust at the naming layer.","sUnion":"The International Confederation of Free Trade Unions applied for .union.","iUnion":"Industrial-era collective institutions were actively looking for a native place in the new digital public sphere.","sNyc":"A .nyc application existed in 2000 and was later withdrawn.","iNyc":"The desire for city identity online appeared before ICANN had a mature framework for city TLDs.","sSucks":"Name.Space proposed .sucks in 2000; a .sucks gTLD was eventually delegated in 2015.","iSucks":"A provocative idea moved from fringe proposal to production infrastructure. The boundary of what “belongs” in the root expanded dramatically.","sWar":".war appeared in the huge Name.Space bundle of proposed strings.","iWar":"The proposal treated the DNS almost as a taxonomy of human life, including its darkest categories.","sSoup":"Yes: .soup was proposed.","iSoup":"The early expansion debate sometimes assumed that almost any semantic category could deserve a top-level namespace.","sMagic":".magic was proposed in 2000.","iMagic":"Early Internet naming still carried a distinctly utopian belief that broad concepts could become self-contained digital places.","sMobi":".mobi was proposed in the 2004 sponsored round and later delegated.","iMobi":"Before responsive design and smartphones normalized one Web, mobile looked different enough to deserve its own namespace.","sMail":".mail was proposed as a sponsored namespace but was not delegated.","iMail":"Spam and trust were already serious enough problems that some actors tried to solve them at the naming layer.","sCat":".cat was created for the Catalan linguistic and cultural community.","iCat":"A TLD did not need to map neatly onto a sovereign state: culture and language could also become infrastructure.","sPost":".post came from the Universal Postal Union.","iPost":"One of the oldest global communications infrastructures tried to map itself into the architecture of the Internet.","sXxx":".xxx became one of the most contentious sponsored-TLD stories.","iXxx":"Naming decisions are not merely technical; they can become proxies for wider political, cultural and regulatory conflicts.","sGuru":".guru was delegated from the 2012 round.","iGuru":"The 2010s turned expertise itself into a marketable online identity.","sNinja":".ninja was delegated from the 2012 round.","iNinja":"The playful job-title vocabulary of startup and developer culture reached the DNS root.","sRich":".rich was delegated from the 2012 round.","iRich":"Status and exclusivity became explicit identity categories that could be bought and signalled online.","sDating":".dating was delegated from the 2012 round.","iDating":"As platforms specialized, intimate life also became a commercial vertical in the namespace.","sGop":".gop was delegated to a US political organization.","iGop":"Political identity itself became a branded namespace, showing how far the 2012 round pushed specialization.","sLgbt":".lgbt was delegated from the 2012 round.","iLgbt":"Community and identity could be represented directly at the top level of the naming system.","sBible":".bible was delegated from the 2012 round.","iBible":"Faith communities joined brands, places and industries in claiming explicit space in the root.","sBeer":".beer was delegated from the 2012 round.","iBeer":"Products were no longer only things sold online; they became lifestyle categories with their own namespaces.","sPizza":".pizza was delegated from the 2012 round.","iPizza":"Everyday consumption became part of the DNS vocabulary, from sectors and brands down to food culture.","sWtf":".wtf was delegated from the 2012 round.","iWtf":"Internet slang itself became infrastructure, perhaps the most concise proof that the namespace had become culturally mainstream.","sFail":".fail was delegated from the 2012 round.","iFail":"Even failure became a viable semantic category in the DNS root. The namespace had moved far beyond institutional labels.","pf2026":"Paid applications officially confirmed as proceeding in the 2026 round.","pf2012apps":"Applications received in the 2012 new-gTLD round.","pfGeo":"2012 applications identified as geographic in ICANN statistics.","pfYears":"Years between the 2012 round and the new 2026 application round.","pfBerlin":".berlin became the first city TLD to open registrations to the general public in 2014.","plan1":"Capture the official APS dataset.","plan2":"Normalize primary, replacement and variant strings.","plan3":"Build contention sets and applicant-level counts.","plan4":"Classify geography/cities, brands, communities, AI/agent, identity/trust, finance/crypto and other editorial themes.","plan5":"Compare with 2000, 2004 and 2012.","plan6":"Generate LinkedIn-ready facts and downloadable data exports.","guard1":"“Geographic” is an ICANN application classification; “city TLD” is a narrower analytical classification used in this publication.","guard2":"Application counts, unique-string counts, registry portfolios, RSP-managed TLDs and delegated-TLD counts are different metrics and are never blended.","guard3":"The 2026 dataset is sourced from ICANN APS. Between Reveal Day and String Confirmation Day, strings and contention sets can still change through the replacement process.","guard4":"“Zeitgeist” themes and the five-layer power model are analytical classifications and remain separate from official ICANN fields.","tabBeyond":"Beyond the dot","tabGeo":"Geography","tabLugano":".lugano","beyondTitle":"When a TLD becomes infrastructure","beyondSub":"Some namespaces went far beyond naming. They added eligibility, verification, security rules, public-interest governance or shared digital services.","maturityTitle":"From a name to a trust layer","maturitySub":"The interesting shift is not the number of domains. It is how much meaning and verification the namespace can carry.","m1":"Name","m1b":"A memorable address.","m2":"Qualified community","m2b":"Rules define who belongs.","m3":"Verified identity","m3b":"Eligibility is checked.","m4":"Security baseline","m4b":"Technical requirements become part of the namespace.","m5":"Shared services","m5b":"The TLD becomes a platform for common services.","stageShared":"Shared services","stageCivic":"Civic infrastructure","stageVerified":"Verified trust","stagePublicId":"Verified public identity","stageQualification":"External qualification","stageCommitment":"Profile + commitment","stageQualified":"Qualified namespace","casePostTitle":"A TLD with a service layer","casePostBody":"The UPU vets registrants, mandates a cybersecurity framework and operates trust.post, a Shared Services Platform for the postal and logistics sector.","caseCatTitle":"A domain that funds a digital commons","caseCatBody":"The .cat registry reinvests its revenues in digital rights, tools and projects for the Catalan-speaking community, describing the wider mission as civic infrastructure.","caseBankTitle":"Security rules encoded into membership","caseBankBody":"Resolving .bank and .insurance domains must implement requirements such as DNSSEC, TLS and email authentication. The suffix is designed to signal a higher-trust environment.","caseGovTitle":"The suffix itself is an institutional credential","caseGovBody":"Only verified U.S. government organizations can register .gov. CISA verifies eligibility and the identity of requesters before approval.","casePharmacyTitle":"A domain tied to accreditation","casePharmacyBody":"A registrant must first demonstrate compliance with NABP standards and receive accreditation. The domain is connected to an external qualification process.","caseEcoTitle":"Activation requires a profile and a pledge","caseEcoBody":"A .eco registration remains on hold until the registrant affirms eligibility, completes an Eco Profile and makes a public environmental commitment.","caseSwissTitle":"A namespace reserved for a defined community","caseSwissBody":".swiss is restricted to the Swiss community and applies eligibility rules linking the registrant and the requested name to Switzerland.","sourceCase":"Primary source","geoTitle":"The geography of power in the DNS","geoSub":"The Internet is physically distributed. No single map shows who ‘controls the Internet’. Rule-making, root coordination, registry contracts, technical capacity and applicant demand cluster in different places; this view keeps those layers separate.","geoPlaces":"Places represented","geoGovernance":"Coordination & root","geoRsp":"Evaluated RSP supply · 2026","geoPlacesNote":"Curated examples of city and territorial TLDs. This layer shows what a namespace represents, not a complete census and not where its technical backend is located.","geoGovernanceNote":"Selected organizational locations for ICANN, PTI/IANA and the Root Zone Maintainer. ICANN policy is a global multistakeholder process; PTI validates root changes and Verisign implements them. Headquarters are not a map of where root service or Internet traffic physically runs.","geoRspNote":"Evaluated RSP organizations by ICANN region as of 31 August 2026: Europe 16, Asia-Pacific 7, North America 6, Africa 0, Latin America & Caribbean 0. This is the geography of pre-evaluated technical supply. Applicants’ selected RSPs are not publicly posted in the 2026 application data (Question 176), so this is not applicant market share.","registryGeoTitle":"Where do TLDs “reside”?","registryGeoBody":"The useful answer has three layers: the place a TLD represents, the legal home of its registry operator, and the distributed technical backend. IANA records the TLD manager for every delegation. A full manager-country aggregation will be added with the 2026 dataset rather than inferred from brand names or server locations.","powerTitle":"Where is DNS power concentrated?","powerBody":"Policy, root-zone management, maintenance, registry operations and technical backends are different roles. Mapping them separately reveals concentration without pretending that one organization “owns the Internet”.","rootStat1":"12 independent root-server operators","rootStat2":"13 logical root-server identities (A–M)","rootStat3":"2,045 operational root-server instances on 30 Sep 2026","rspStat":"29 evaluated RSP organizations in August 2026","mapHint":"Select a layer to explore the map. Hover or tap a marker for context.","nodeLA":"ICANN headquarters and Public Technical Identifiers (IANA functions).","nodeReston":"Verisign headquarters; Verisign performs Root Zone Maintainer services.","nodeRegional":"ICANN regional office.","nodeEngagement":"ICANN engagement center.","luganoTitle":".lugano — from a name to a trust layer","luganoSub":"A vision for what the namespace could become if the application succeeds. This is direction, not an announcement of services already implemented.","visionBadge":"VISION · NOT YET IMPLEMENTED","luganoLead":"The ambition is not simply to put Lugano after a dot. It is to create a public-interest namespace in which trust can become visible, governable and eventually machine-readable.","lugPillar1":"Public stewardship","lugPillar1b":"Rules, reserved names and strategic choices remain under public responsibility.","lugPillar2":"Qualified participation","lugPillar2b":"Different classes of users or services could carry explicit eligibility and accountability rules.","lugPillar3":"Security & interoperability","lugPillar3b":"Minimum technical requirements and open interfaces can make trust operational rather than merely symbolic.","lugPillar4":"Machine-readable trust","lugPillar4b":"In the longer term, software and AI agents could verify who they are interacting with, what a service is allowed to do and which rules apply.","lugRoadmap":"An illustrative progression","lugRoadmapSub":"Each step would require its own governance, legal and technical decisions after a successful ICANN process.","lugStage1":"Secure registry foundation","lugStage1b":"Registry policies, reserved names, institutional domains, abuse handling and operational continuity.","lugStage2":"Verified ecosystem","lugStage2b":"Qualified organizations, services and local actors with clear conditions of use and transparent responsibilities.","lugStage3":"Digital commons layer","lugStage3b":"Shared technical components, metadata and interoperable services that reduce friction for the local ecosystem.","lugStage4":"Agent-ready trust","lugStage4b":"Machine-readable identity, delegation and service discovery so intelligent systems can evaluate trust as well as humans do.","luganoGuard":"Nothing in this section implies that ICANN has assigned .lugano or that these services already exist. It describes a possible design direction, subject to the application outcome and future decisions by the City.","geoSourceLine":"Method: policy, root coordination, contracts, technical backend and demand are separate layers. Geographic concentration is evidence of asymmetry, not proof that one place or company “controls the Internet”.","beyondQuote":"Some of the clearest examples show that a TLD can encode more than a name: eligibility, identity, security, community rules and shared services.","eyebrow":"ICANN · DNS · CITIES · SOCIETY","fact":"FACT","reading":"READING","vision":"VISION","legendText":"verified information · interpretation of the data · future scenario","delegationRecords":"delegation records · 2012 round","delegationCaveat":"ICANN reports 1,241 delegations from the 2012 round; this total is not adjusted for TLDs later terminated or removed from the root zone.","source2012":"ICANN 2012 statistics","sourceReveal":"ICANN Reveal Day","mobileNavigate":"Navigate","roundAtGlance":"Rounds at a glance","roundAtGlanceSub":"Four openings, radically different scales. Values are application counts, not the number of TLDs ultimately delegated.","applicantRegion2012":"Applicant demand · 2012","applicantRegion2012Note":"Official ICANN regional distribution of the 1,930 applications, based on the applicant’s principal place of business. This is not where a TLD “lives”.","rspEvaluated":"evaluated RSPs","knownReveal":"What Reveal Day will tell us","unknownReveal":"What it will not tell us yet","knownRevealSub":"At 18:00 UTC on 7 October, ICANN will publish the public-facing application data.","unknownRevealSub":"Reveal is the beginning of evaluation, not the assignment of a TLD.","known1":"Applied-for primary strings and the applicants behind them.","known2":"Applicable variant or replacement strings.","known3":"Initial contention sets.","known4":"Public portions of each application.","unknown1":"Which applications will pass all evaluations.","unknown2":"Who will prevail in unresolved contention sets.","unknown3":"When a successful TLD will be delegated or launch.","unknown4":"That .lugano has been assigned: it remains an application until the process is completed.","revealLocal":"Reveal Day: 7 October 2026 · 18:00 UTC · 20:00 in Lugano","curatedSample":"curated sample","downloadExcel":"Complete Excel workbook","excelSub":"XLSX · publication-ready tables + sources","dataPackSub":"ZIP · Excel workbook + all CSV datasets","footerLeftV05":"Pre-Reveal snapshot · updated 2 October 2026 · 1984–2026","footerRightV05":"Primary sources: ICANN, IANA and cited registry / governance sources","roleTagPolicy":"POLICY","roleTagRoot":"ROOT","roleTagGovernance":"GOVERNANCE","roleTagTech":"TECH","roleTagRetail":"RETAIL","roleTagUser":"USER","searchLabel":"Search Strange Internet","roundFilterLabel":"Round","categoryFilterLabel":"Filter by category","mapLabel":"Map layer","methodLegendTitle":"How to read this publication","methodLegendBody":"FACT marks verifiable information supported by a cited source. READING marks our interpretation of the data or historical record. VISION marks a future scenario, direction or proposal. Project status is shown separately because status and epistemic nature are not the same thing.","downloadGeoRegions":"2012 applicant geography","downloadGeoRegionsSub":"CSV · official ICANN regional totals","beyondDownload":"Beyond the dot","rspDownload":"RSP geography","secondarySource":"SECONDARY SOURCE","statusApplication2026":"2026 APPLICATION","statusNotImplemented":"NOT YET IMPLEMENTED","luganoStatusTitle":"Current status","luganoStatusBody":"The City of Lugano has submitted an application for .lugano in the ICANN 2026 round. An application is not an assignment or a delegation: .lugano becomes active only after the applicable evaluation, contracting and root-zone delegation steps.","operator":"Operator source","awaitReveal":"OFFICIAL 2026 DATA · TO LOAD","awaitRevealShort":"Official 2026 data to load","afterReveal":"2026 ANALYSIS","provisionalUntilConfirmation":"PROVISIONAL UNTIL 17 NOV","homeUnique2026":"unique primary strings · 2026","homeApplicants2026":"applicant organizations · 2026","homeContention2026":"initial contention sets · 2026","homeBenchmark2012":"2012 benchmark: 1,930 applications · ≈1,400 unique strings · 230 exact-match contention sets · 66 geographic applications.","round2026Title":"The 2026 round","round2026Sub":"Before Reveal Day, only confirmed pre-Reveal figures are shown as facts. The remaining 2026 fields are reserved for the official ICANN application dataset published on 7 October.","round2026Snapshot":"Confirmed before Reveal Day","round2026Unique":"unique primary strings","round2026Applicants":"applicant organizations","round2026Contention":"initial contention sets","round2026Geo":"geographic applications","round2026Idn":"IDN / variant applications","dataStatusTitle":"Reveal Day will be a snapshot, not the final string list","dataStatusSub":"ICANN will publish the initial dataset on 7 October. Eligible applicants can switch to a replacement string from 8 to 21 October; ICANN is scheduled to publish the confirmed string list and updated contention sets on 17 November.","milestoneReveal":"7 OCT · REVEAL DAY","milestoneRevealBody":"Applicants, primary strings, applicable variants/replacements, initial contention sets and public application data.","milestoneReplacement":"8–21 OCT · REPLACEMENT PERIOD","milestoneReplacementBody":"Eligible applicants may switch from their primary string to a replacement string.","milestoneConfirm":"17 NOV · STRING CONFIRMATION","milestoneConfirmBody":"Final applied-for string list and updated contention sets.","round2026Patterns":"What the 2026 data can tell us","round2026PatternsSub":"The same official dataset can be read from several angles rather than reduced to a single league table.","patternGeo":"Where demand comes from","patternGeoBody":"Regions, countries and concentration of applicant organizations, derived from their public business location.","patternApplicants":"Who is applying","patternApplicantsBody":"Legal applicants, parent groups, repeat applicants, brands, communities, geographies and portfolio strategies.","patternWords":"What the new Internet wants to call itself","patternWordsBody":"Semantic clusters, unusual strings and the social or technological vocabulary of 2026.","patternCompetition":"Where scarcity remains","patternCompetitionBody":"Contention sets, repeated strings and areas where many applicants converged on the same idea.","geoLayer2026":"Geographic layer · 2026","geoApps2026":"geographic applications in the 2026 round","geoApps2026Note":"After Reveal Day, the official value will be derived from the ICANN APS dataset.","geoCities2026":"city / territorial strings in 2026","geoCities2026Note":"Classification derived from the public 2026 application data.","powerStackTitle":"Power is a stack, not a dot on a map","powerStackSub":"Five layers matter, and they describe different things. Collapsing them into one “center of the Internet” would be neat, dramatic and wrong.","powerRules":"Rules","powerRulesBody":"ICANN’s global multistakeholder process develops policy; ICANN org implements the contractual and program framework for gTLDs.","powerRoot":"Root coordination","powerRootBody":"PTI validates root-zone change requests; Verisign implements validated changes as Root Zone Maintainer. Root serving itself is distributed among independent operators worldwide.","powerContracts":"Registry contracts","powerContractsBody":"Each gTLD registry operator holds contractual responsibility for its namespace. Some operators hold large portfolios of registry agreements.","powerBackend":"Technical backend","powerBackendBody":"Registry Service Providers supply registry, DNS and DNSSEC infrastructure across many TLDs. Technical concentration can differ sharply from ownership.","powerDemand":"Demand","powerDemandBody":"Applicants decide which strings are worth pursuing. Their public business locations reveal where commercial, civic and institutional demand originates.","geoMapTitle":"One map, four different questions","geoMapSub":"Switch layers to see institutional coordination, evaluated technical supply and applicant demand. They overlap, but they are not the same kind of power.","applicantRegion2026":"Applicant demand · 2026","applicantRegion2026Note":"After Reveal Day, this distribution will be derived from applicants’ public primary business locations in the official 2026 dataset. It will map applicant organizations, not users, DNS infrastructure or the geography of final economic activity.","demand2012Title":"Applicant demand · 2012","demand2012Sub":"ICANN recorded 1,930 applications from 60 countries and territories. North America and Europe together accounted for 82.2% of them.","demand2012Concentration":"82.2% · North America + Europe","rspSupplyTitle":"Technical supply · evaluated RSPs","rspSupplySub":"By August 2026, 29 organizations had cleared at least one RSP evaluation. Europe alone accounted for 16, or 55.2%.","rspEuropeShare":"55.2% · Europe","demand2026Title":"Applicant demand · 2026","demand2026Sub":"After Reveal Day this view will be derived from public primary business locations in the official 2026 dataset. It will describe applicant demand, not infrastructure geography.","topCountries2026Title":"Top applicant countries · 2026","topCountries2026Sub":"After Reveal Day, this ranking will be derived from applicants’ public primary business locations in the official 2026 dataset.","identityTitle":"Who built the broadest TLD portfolio? Donuts → Identity Digital","identitySub":"Donuts was by far the largest applicant in 2012, filing 307 applications through subsidiaries. The business later expanded through acquisitions and now operates as Identity Digital, which ICANN describes as the world’s largest TLD registry operator.","identityApps":"applications filed by Donuts in 2012","identityPortfolio":"TLDs in Identity Digital’s current portfolio","identityManaged":"TLDs Identity Digital says it manages technically","identityHQ":"headquarters · Bellevue, Washington","identityPortfolioClaim":"Identity Digital describes a portfolio of nearly 300 TLDs. It also says it technically manages 460+ gTLDs, ccTLDs and dotBrands as a Registry Services Provider. Those are different measures: portfolio ownership / operation is not the same as technical backend management.","identityT2012":"2012 · 307 applications","identityT2017":"2017 · Rightside acquired","identityT2020":"2020 · Afilias acquired","identityT2022":"2022 · Identity Digital brand","identityT2026":"2026 · ~300 portfolio TLDs / 460+ managed","identityReadingTitle":"Why does concentration emerge?","identityReadingBody":"Registry operations carry substantial fixed costs in infrastructure, security, compliance and registrar distribution. A broad portfolio lets an operator reuse the same backend and sales channels, spread risk across niche strings and grow further through acquisitions. Donuts explicitly cited economies of scale as a core reason for applying for hundreds of strings in 2012.","identityCaveat":"Applications ≠ awarded TLDs ≠ registry agreements ≠ TLD portfolio ≠ RSP-managed TLDs ≠ Internet governance. The numbers belong to different layers and must not be added together.","identity2026Top":"Largest legal applicant · 2026","identity2026TopSub":"After Reveal Day, this will be calculated from the official 2026 dataset. Legal applicants will be counted as published; a separate view may group applications by publicly disclosed direct / ultimate parent where the data supports it.","rootDistributedTitle":"The root is coordinated through a small number of institutions, but served globally","rootDistributedBody":"PTI manages root-zone changes and Verisign maintains the root-zone file, while 12 independent root-server operators serve 13 logical root identities through thousands of physical instances worldwide. Institutional coordination and physical delivery therefore have very different geographies.","geoInterpretTitle":"What “Internet power” means here","geoInterpretBody":"The dashboard can show where policy institutions, root functions, registry contracts, applicant headquarters and technical providers cluster. It cannot reduce Internet governance to headquarters, or equate a large TLD portfolio with control over ICANN policy or the DNS root.","contentionTitle2026":"Where applicants collide in 2026","contention2026Sub":"Initial contention sets will be available at Reveal Day. They may still change through replacement strings and later string-confusion processes; 17 November will provide a cleaner comparison point.","contention2026Label":"initial contention sets · 2026","contention2012Title":"The 2012 benchmark","contentionCompareTitle":"Competition is a cultural signal too","contentionCompareBody":"The most contested strings show where many applicants independently saw the same future market. Comparing 2012 and 2026 turns contention from a procedural detail into a measure of changing expectations.","downloadRound2026":"2026 round data (CSV)","downloadRound2026Sub":"CSV · official 2026 metrics, method and sources","downloadConcentration":"Registry concentration","downloadConcentrationSub":"CSV · Donuts / Identity Digital chronology, metrics and sources","downloadGeo2026":"2026 applicant geography","downloadGeo2026Sub":"CSV · applicant geography derived from public 2026 application data","homeCountries2026":"countries & territories represented · 2026","round2026Countries":"countries & territories represented","round2026Community":"community applications","round2026Brand":".Brand applications","identityGap":"In 2012 Donuts filed 307 applications, more than three times Google’s 101, the next-largest applicant; Amazon filed 78.","identity2026TopGroup":"Largest corporate group · 2026","identity2026GroupSub":"After Reveal Day, corporate-group normalization will use only public parent-company information in ICANN applications and will remain distinct from the legal-applicant ranking.","downloadRegions2026":"2026 applicant geography (CSV)","downloadRegistryConcentration":"Registry concentration case (CSV)","scaleContrastTitle":"Two different forms of concentration","scaleContrastSub":"Counting TLD strings and counting domains registered underneath them produces two very different pictures of scale.","identityBreadthLabel":"TLDs in Identity Digital’s portfolio · breadth across namespaces","verisignDepthLabel":".com + .net registrations · Q2 2026","verisignInfraLabel":"Root Zone Maintainer · operator of A-root and J-root","scaleContrastReading":"There is no single league table of “Internet power”. Identity Digital concentrates portfolio breadth across many TLDs; Verisign combines exceptional registration depth under .com/.net with important root operational roles. Neither measure is equivalent to control over ICANN policy.","applications2026Download":"2026 application-level data (CSV)","applications2026DownloadSub":"CSV · normalized schema for the official public 2026 application dataset","strange2026Pending":"2026 application data are part of the publication structure. This working build is waiting for the official ICANN dataset; the published post-Reveal version will populate this layer with real strings and sources.","tabExplore":"Explore","exploreTitle":"Namespace Explorer · preview","exploreSub":"A curated search across selected active TLDs, historical applications and the emerging 2026 dataset. Absence from this preview does not mean a string does not exist.","exploreCoverageTitle":"Preview corpus","exploreCoverageBody":"This build indexes a curated seed corpus while the full IANA root and the official 2026 application dataset are prepared for import. Coverage is intentionally incomplete and is labelled as such.","exploreIana":"active TLDs in the IANA root snapshot","exploreSeed":"records indexed in this build","exploreSearchPlaceholder":"Search a TLD, applicant, place or theme…","exploreSearchHint":"Search uses names plus editorial tags, so “trust”, “city” or “identity” can return related records.","exploreRound":"Round","exploreStatus":"Status","exploreType":"Type","exploreTheme":"Theme","allStatuses":"All statuses","allTypes":"All types","allThemes":"All themes","presetAll":"All","preset2026":"2026","presetStrange":"Strange","presetCities":"Cities & places","presetContention":"Contention","presetTrust":"Trust & identity","exploreResults":"records","exploreNoResults":"No matching records.","exploreCorpusNote":"Coverage note","exploreCorpusNoteBody":"This is a curated preview, not a complete namespace index. After Reveal Day, the official 2026 application dataset will be imported and coverage will expand without changing the interface.","exploreColTld":"TLD / string","exploreColStatus":"Status","exploreColRound":"Round","exploreColEntity":"Applicant / manager","exploreColGeo":"Geography","exploreColType":"Type","exploreColTheme":"Theme","exploreOpen":"Open record","exploreFactFields":"Verified fields","exploreReadingFields":"Editorial reading","exploreSources":"Sources","exploreClose":"Close","exploreApplicantManager":"Applicant / manager","exploreGroup":"Corporate group","exploreGeography":"Geography","exploreContention":"Applications in contention","exploreContext":"Context","exploreReading":"Interpretation","typeApplication":"Application","typeGeneric":"Generic","typeSponsored":"Sponsored / restricted","typeInfrastructure":"Infrastructure","typeGeographic":"Geographic","typeBrand":"Brand","typeCommunity":"Community","typeCountryCode":"Country-code","legacy":"Legacy","application":"Application","business":"Business","technology":"Technology","education":"Education","government":"Government","infrastructure":"Infrastructure","ai":"AI & agents","preset":"Preset","strangeExplorerCta":"Explore these and the full namespace","strangeExplorerNote":"Strange Internet is now an editorial lens. Search and filtering live in the Explorer, where these records sit beside the rest of the namespace.","searchLabelExplorer":"Search namespace","statusFilterLabel":"Filter by status","typeFilterLabel":"Filter by type","themeFilterLabel":"Filter by theme","explorePlaceholderBadge":"PROVISIONAL DATASET","exploreIanaDate":"IANA snapshot · 2 Oct 2026","projectByline":"Independent research project by <strong>Robert Bregy</strong>.","projectDisclaimer":"This project does not represent an official position of the City of Lugano.","footerAttribution":"Research, analysis and editorial interpretation by Robert Bregy.","navThemes":"Themes","navRound":"Round 2026","navResearch":"Research","publicationState":"PRE-REVEAL SNAPSHOT · 2 OCT 2026","publicationStateNote":"Reveal Day · 7 Oct 2026 · 18:00 UTC","exploreLoadMore":"Load more","exploreShowing":"showing {shown} of {total}","roundAwaitingTitle":"Available on Reveal Day · 7 October 2026","roundAwaitingBody":"The official application dataset will populate unique strings, applicant organizations, countries and territories, contention sets, geographic/community/.Brand applications, and IDN/variant fields.","luganoVisionCondition":"VISION · IF DELEGATED","shareLabel":"Share this view","themeLabel":"Toggle theme","languageLabel":"Language","closeLabel":"Close explorer record","drawerLabel":"Explorer record","mapItemLabel":"Map item: {name}","resourcesNav":"Resources","methodNav":"Methodology & sources","metaDescription":"From .arpa to .lugano: how Internet domains became identity, infrastructure and trust. An independent research publication by Robert Bregy.","metaOgDescription":"What we put after the dot says something about the world we imagine.","tocLabel":"On this page","homeLabel":"Go to Overview","sectionNavLabel":"Sections","explorerPresetsLabel":"Explorer presets","timelineStageProtocol":"PROTOCOL","timelineStageRoot":"ROOT MILESTONE","timelineStageIdea":"IDEA","timelineStageRound":"APPLICATION ROUND","timelineStageLaunch":"DELEGATION / LAUNCH"},"it":{"brand":"Brand","copyLink":"Copia link","copied":"Copiato","heroTitle":"Connecting the Dots","heroBody":"Da .arpa a .lugano: come i domini Internet sono diventati identità, infrastruttura e fiducia.","tabOverview":"Panoramica","tabArchaeology":"Archeologia","tabHow":"Come funziona","tabCities":"Città e territori","tabContention":"Contese","tabStrange":"Internet strano","tabReveal":"Round 2026","tabPress":"Dati & fonti","tabSources":"Metodologia","applicationsRound":"Candidature per round","applicationsRoundSub":"Il salto del 2012 ha cambiato la scala dello spazio dei nomi.","icann2012":"2012: cosa ricevette ICANN","icann2012Sub":"Sono classificazioni ufficiali distinte, non fette della stessa torta.","overviewCallout":"Quello che mettiamo dopo il punto dice qualcosa sul mondo che immaginiamo.","overviewFoot":"I campi ufficiali ICANN restano separati dalla lettura editoriale. L’obiettivo è leggere la storia senza trasformarla in marketing.","archTitle":"Una breve archeologia dell’espansione","archSub":"Alcune tappe sono round ICANN; altre spiegano come sono diventati possibili i domini cittadini.","timeCapsule":"Il 2000 sembra già una capsula del tempo","timeCapsuleSub":"Una selezione dal round sperimentale.","string":"Stringa","theme":"Tema","status":"Stato","why":"Perché conta","scopeGeneral":"Principi generali","scopeGeneralSub":"Come funzionano DNS, ruoli dei TLD e processo per i nuovi gTLD, indipendentemente da .lugano.","scopeLugano":".lugano · caso applicato","scopeLuganoSub":"Cosa significano questi principi generali per la candidatura di Lugano nel round 2026.","luganoAppliedTitle":"Dalla regola generale al caso .lugano","luganoAppliedBody":"La candidatura .lugano segue lo stesso percorso descritto sopra. Finché valutazione, contratto e delegazione non sono completati, .lugano resta una candidatura e non è un TLD attivo.","luganoExampleLabel":"Esempio ipotetico","luganoExampleBody":"Se .lugano sarà delegato, la stessa struttura DNS potrà produrre indirizzi come servizi.lugano o www.servizi.lugano. L’esempio illustra la gerarchia; non indica un servizio già attivo.","luganoLanguageGuide":"Parlare con precisione di .lugano","luganoLanguageGuideSub":"Due distinzioni importanti finché la candidatura è ancora in corso.","howTitle":"Come funzionano davvero i domini Internet","howSub":"I concetti essenziali per orientarsi tra domini, DNS e governance di Internet.","anatomyTitle":"Partiamo da un indirizzo","anatomyBody":"In <b>www.example.com</b>, <b>.com</b> è il dominio di primo livello (TLD). “example” è il nome registrato direttamente sotto quel dominio. “www” è un’etichetta opzionale aggiunta davanti al nome registrato.","rootLabel":"Radice","rootBody":"Il livello più alto, normalmente invisibile, della gerarchia DNS.","tldLabel":"Dominio di primo livello","tldBody":"La parte finale: .ch, .com, .org.","secondLabel":"Nome registrato","secondBody":"Il nome immediatamente a sinistra del TLD, per esempio example.com.","hostLabel":"Etichetta opzionale","hostBody":"Un’etichetta opzionale aggiunta davanti al nome registrato. In www.example.com, “www” può identificare un sito o un servizio specifico; non fa parte del TLD.","whoTitle":"Chi fa cosa?","whoSub":"Internet non ha un unico “proprietario”. Funziona attraverso ruoli distinti, contratti e coordinamento tecnico.","roleIcann":"ICANN","roleIcannBody":"Coordina politiche e contratti dei domini generici di primo livello attraverso un modello globale multistakeholder.","roleIana":"IANA / PTI","roleIanaBody":"Mantiene il database autorevole della root e coordina tecnicamente la delegazione dei TLD nel DNS.","roleRegistry":"Operatore del registro","roleRegistryBody":"Gestisce le regole e il database autorevole di un TLD. Il registry operator è responsabile del funzionamento di quello spazio di nomi nel quadro contrattuale ICANN.","roleRsp":"Registry Service Provider (RSP)","roleRspBody":"Fornisce i servizi tecnici critici del registro, come DNS e infrastruttura di registrazione.","roleRegistrar":"Registrar","roleRegistrarBody":"È il livello commerciale/intermediario attraverso il quale normalmente si registrano e gestiscono i nomi di dominio.","roleRegistrant":"Titolare (registrant)","roleRegistrantBody":"È la persona o organizzazione che registra e utilizza un nome sotto il TLD.","birthTitle":"Come nasce un nuovo TLD","birthSub":"Il processo 2026 non è “faccio domanda e lo ricevo”. È una procedura pubblica a tappe.","step1":"Candidatura","step1b":"Il candidato presenta la domanda e paga la tassa prevista.","step2":"Reveal","step2b":"ICANN pubblica i dati pubblici delle candidature e individua le contese per stringhe identiche.","step3":"Osservazioni e obiezioni","step3b":"Entrano nel processo contributi della comunità, obiezioni formali ed eventuali pareri governativi rilevanti.","step4":"Valutazioni","step4b":"Vengono valutati stringa, candidato, capacità operative e finanziarie e componenti tecniche.","step5":"Contese","step5b":"Se più candidati chiedono la stessa stringa o stringhe confondibili, la contesa deve essere risolta.","step6":"Contratto","step6b":"Il candidato che supera il processo firma il Base Registry Agreement con ICANN.","step7":"Delegazione","step7b":"Dopo onboarding e test tecnici, IANA/PTI può inserire il TLD nella root DNS.","step8":"Avvio","step8b":"Solo a quel punto il registro può procedere verso l’uso effettivo e l’assegnazione dei nomi sotto il TLD.","controlTitle":"Nel caso .lugano: cosa potrebbe governare il registro e cosa non significherebbe","canTitle":"Cosa potrebbe governare il registro","can1":"Criteri di ammissibilità e assegnazione dei nomi sotto .lugano, nel rispetto delle regole ICANN e della legge applicabile.","can2":"Nomi riservati, fasi di lancio e scelte di governance locale.","can3":"Requisiti di registrazione, tecnici e di sicurezza per i nomi che operano sotto .lugano.","cannotTitle":"Cosa non significherebbe","cannot1":"Lugano non “possiede Internet” e non controlla .ch, .com o altri TLD.","cannot2":"Una candidatura non è un’assegnazione: .lugano non è attivo finché processo, contratto e delegazione non sono completati.","cannot3":"ICANN non gestisce i siti della Città: coordina il sistema globale dei nomi e il quadro contrattuale dei gTLD.","sourceGuide":"Fonte ufficiale","cityTitle":"Dal nome di luogo all’infrastruttura pubblica","citySub":"L’idea dei city-TLD precede il round 2012 di oltre un decennio.","geoLayer":"Lo strato geografico del 2012","geoApps":"candidature identificate come geografiche","geoDelegated":"gTLD geografici delegati dal round 2012 nelle statistiche ICANN","secondGen":"Una seconda generazione di city-TLD?","secondGenBody":"La prima ondata riguardava soprattutto identità, appartenenza territoriale e registrazione. Nel 2026 uno spazio cittadino può essere progettato anche attorno a servizi pubblici affidabili, identità verificabile, governance leggibile dalle macchine e interazione con agenti AI. È una nostra ipotesi editoriale, non una classificazione ICANN.","contentionTitle":"Quello che tutti volevano nel 2012","contentionSub":"Le contese sono una fotografia sorprendentemente efficace dell’epoca delle app, delle piattaforme e dell’e-commerce.","strangeTitle":"The Strange Internet","strangeSub":"Non solo nomi buffi. Sono piccoli fossili di ciò che ogni epoca riteneva abbastanza importante da meritare un pezzo di Internet.","searchPlaceholder":"Cerca .wtf, mobile, identità, fiducia…","allRounds":"Tutti i round","crossRound":"Tra più round","allCategories":"Tutte le categorie","reset":"Azzera","searchHint":"Cerca un TLD, un tema sociale o un’idea.","results":"risultati","noResults":"Nessun risultato. Perfino Internet, a volte, esaurisce le stranezze.","source":"Fonte","sourcesN":"Fonti","whatItWas":"Cos’era","whatItSays":"Cosa racconta della sua epoca","strangeNext":"<b>Livello 2026:</b> dopo il Reveal Day, le candidature ufficiali saranno classificate con la stessa disciplina del materiale storico: il record della candidatura è un FATTO; il significato culturale o tecnologico che ne ricaviamo è una LETTURA.","official2026":"Stato ufficiale 2026","paidApps":"candidature pagate che procedono","revealBody":"ICANN pubblicherà i dati pubblici delle candidature il 7 ottobre 2026 alle 18:00 UTC, comprese le stringhe primarie e le contese per stringhe identiche.","preRevealIntel":"Informazioni pre-Reveal","disclosed":"stringhe rese pubbliche volontariamente e tracciate da TLDz al 26 settembre 2026","secondaryNote":"Fonte secondaria. Non è l’elenco ufficiale ICANN e sarà sostituita dai dati del Reveal Day.","importPlan":"Piano di importazione del Reveal Day","pressTitle":"Dati, fatti e risorse riutilizzabili","pressSub":"Fatti verificati, definizioni e risorse riutilizzabili per leggere i dati senza attribuire loro più di quanto dicano.","safeFacts":"Cinque fatti chiave","copyFact":"Copia","languageGuide":"Parole precise, concetti chiari","languageGuideSub":"I concetti essenziali per orientarsi tra domini, DNS e governance di Internet.","sayThis":"Più preciso","notThis":"Da non confondere con","safe1":"La Città di Lugano ha presentato la candidatura per il nuovo dominio di primo livello .lugano.","avoid1":"Lugano ha ottenuto / possiede il dominio .lugano.","safe2":"Un dominio di primo livello è la parte finale di un indirizzo Internet, come .ch o .com.","avoid2":"Un TLD è semplicemente un nuovo indirizzo web.","safe3":"ICANN coordina il sistema globale dei nomi di dominio e il processo per i nuovi gTLD.","avoid3":"ICANN è il governo o il proprietario di Internet.","safe4":"Se la candidatura avrà successo, .lugano potrà diventare uno spazio regolato secondo criteri definiti dal suo registry operator nel quadro ICANN.","avoid4":"Chiunque potrà automaticamente registrare qualsiasi nome .lugano.","downloadsTitle":"Scarica i dati di lavoro","downloadsSub":"I dataset possono essere scaricati per verifica, riutilizzo o ulteriori analisi.","downloadStrange":"Dataset Strange Internet","downloadRounds":"Sintesi dei round","downloadContention":"Contese 2012","downloadGlossary":"Glossario DNS e governance","downloadFacts":"Fatti chiave","downloadPack":"Scarica tutto il pacchetto dati (.zip)","anglesTitle":"Domande che emergono dai dati","angle1":"Come il linguaggio delle candidature TLD riflette la società di ogni epoca.","angle2":"Da .berlin a .lugano: come le città sono entrate nella root DNS.","angle3":"Perché “candidatura” e “delegazione” sono due momenti molto diversi.","angle4":"Cosa raccontano le contese del 2026 su AI, agenti, finanza, brand e identità.","angle5":"Un city-TLD può evolvere da strumento di identità digitale a infrastruttura di fiducia?","sourcesTitle":"Fonti e metodologia","sourcesSub":"Preferenza alle fonti primarie. Le fonti secondarie sono indicate esplicitamente.","guardrails":"Paletti metodologici","primary":"Primaria","secondary":"Secondaria","footerLeft":"Prototipo pre-Reveal · dati congelati al 1° ottobre 2026","footerRight":"Statico, local-first e pronto per la pubblicazione web.","applications":"candidature","applicants":"candidati","geographic":"Geografiche","community":"Community","idn":"IDN","round2000":"Prova di concetto","round2004":"Round sponsored TLD","round2012":"Espansione di massa","round2026":"Nuovo round","tl1984Title":"Prima di .com c’era .arpa","tl1984Body":"RFC 920 descriveva .arpa come dominio di primo livello iniziale e transitorio dell’ARPA-Internet durante l’introduzione del DNS. Doveva scomparire; è invece sopravvissuto e oggi serve funzioni infrastrutturali di Internet.","tl1985Title":"Il primo .com: symbolics.com","tl1985Body":"Nel gennaio 1985 .com, .edu, .gov, .mil, .net e .org entrarono nella root insieme a .arpa. Il 15 marzo symbolics.com divenne il primo dominio .com registrato.","tl1999Title":"Compare l’idea della città","tl1999Body":"L’idea di .berlin viene fatta risalire al 1999.","tl2000Title":"Prova di concetto","tl2000Body":"47 candidature. Compare persino .nyc, poi ritirata.","tl2004Title":"Comunità sponsorizzate","tl2004Body":"10 candidature per 9 stringhe: .asia, .cat, .jobs, .mail, .mobi, .post, .tel, .travel e .xxx.","tl2012Title":"L’esplosione","tl2012Body":"1.930 candidature, circa 1.400 stringhe uniche e 66 candidature geografiche.","tl2014Title":"Le città vanno online","tl2014Body":".berlin apre al pubblico; .nyc e .paris vengono delegate.","tl2026Title":"Il prossimo strato","tl2026Body":"1.616 candidature pagate procedono. Reveal Day: 7 ottobre 2026. Entra in scena .lugano.","city":"Città","children":"Minori","health":"Salute","labour":"Lavoro","publicInterest":"Interesse pubblico","techFossil":"Fossile tecnologico","geography":"Geografia","culture":"Cultura","identity":"Identità","publicInfrastructure":"Infrastruttura pubblica","oddity":"Stranezza","socialMirror":"Specchio sociale","optimism":"Ottimismo","trust":"Fiducia","adult":"Adulti","personalBrand":"Brand personale","statusCulture":"Status","intimacy":"Intimità","politics":"Politica","faith":"Fede","lifestyle":"Stile di vita","protest":"Contestazione","withdrawn":"Ritirata","returned":"Restituita","evaluated":"Valutata","selected":"Selezionata","proposed":"Proposta","delegated":"Delegata","notDelegated":"Non delegato","longitudinal":"Proposta → delegata","cNyc":"Una candidatura city-TLD esisteva già nel 2000.","cKids":"Più soggetti volevano uno spazio dei nomi dedicato ai bambini.","cHealth":"L’Organizzazione mondiale della sanità propose uno spazio .health.","cUnion":"Il movimento sindacale internazionale presentò .union.","cWap":"Un fossile puro dell’epoca WAP e del “web mobile” separato.","cGeo":"Un’idea di namespace geografico ben prima dell’ondata cittadina del 2012.","cMuseum":"Uno dei sette TLD selezionati nel proof of concept.","cName":"L’identità personale immaginata direttamente nello spazio dei nomi.","cPost":"Infrastruttura di servizio pubblico; il settore postale tornò con .post nel 2004.","sKids":"Nel 2000 più candidati proposero .kids; una candidatura lo abbinava addirittura a .xxx.","iKids":"Il primo web si chiedeva già se i bambini avessero bisogno di uno spazio distinto e governato, non soltanto di contenuti filtrati.","sHealth":"L’OMS propose un namespace dedicato .health.","iHealth":"Molto prima delle discussioni odierne sulla disinformazione sanitaria, attori pubblici pensavano già alla fiducia a livello di naming.","sUnion":"La Confederazione internazionale dei sindacati liberi presentò .union.","iUnion":"Le istituzioni collettive dell’era industriale cercavano attivamente un luogo nativo nella nuova sfera pubblica digitale.","sNyc":"Una candidatura .nyc esisteva già nel 2000 e fu poi ritirata.","iNyc":"Il desiderio di identità cittadina online emerge prima che ICANN disponga di un quadro maturo per i city-TLD.","sSucks":"Name.Space propose .sucks nel 2000; un vero gTLD .sucks venne poi delegato nel 2015.","iSucks":"Un’idea provocatoria passa dalla periferia del dibattito a infrastruttura reale. Il confine di ciò che “merita” la root si allarga enormemente.","sWar":".war compariva nell’enorme pacchetto di stringhe proposto da Name.Space.","iWar":"La proposta trattava quasi il DNS come una tassonomia dell’intera esperienza umana, comprese le categorie più oscure.","sSoup":"Sì: fu proposta anche .soup.","iSoup":"Nel dibattito iniziale sull’espansione sembrava plausibile che quasi ogni categoria semantica potesse meritare un dominio di primo livello.","sMagic":".magic fu proposta nel 2000.","iMagic":"Il naming del primo Internet conservava una vena utopica: grandi concetti come luoghi digitali autonomi.","sMobi":".mobi fu proposta nel round sponsored del 2004 e poi delegata.","iMobi":"Prima che responsive design e smartphone rendessero naturale un unico web, il mobile sembrava abbastanza diverso da meritare un namespace proprio.","sMail":".mail fu proposta come namespace sponsored ma non venne delegata.","iMail":"Spam e fiducia erano già problemi abbastanza seri da tentare una soluzione a livello del naming.","sCat":".cat fu creato per la comunità linguistica e culturale catalana.","iCat":"Un TLD non doveva coincidere con uno Stato: anche lingua e cultura potevano diventare infrastruttura.","sPost":".post nacque dall’Unione postale universale.","iPost":"Una delle più antiche infrastrutture globali di comunicazione cercava di tradursi nell’architettura di Internet.","sXxx":".xxx divenne una delle storie più controverse dei sponsored TLD.","iXxx":"Le scelte di naming non sono solo tecniche: possono diventare il terreno di conflitti culturali, politici e regolatori più ampi.","sGuru":".guru fu delegata dal round 2012.","iGuru":"Negli anni Dieci perfino l’expertise diventa un’identità online commercializzabile.","sNinja":".ninja fu delegata dal round 2012.","iNinja":"Il vocabolario giocoso di startup e sviluppatori arrivò fino alla root DNS.","sRich":".rich fu delegata dal round 2012.","iRich":"Status ed esclusività diventano categorie identitarie esplicite da acquistare e segnalare online.","sDating":".dating fu delegata dal round 2012.","iDating":"Con la specializzazione delle piattaforme, anche la vita intima diventa una verticale commerciale nello spazio dei nomi.","sGop":".gop fu delegata a un’organizzazione politica statunitense.","iGop":"Anche l’identità politica diventa namespace di marca, mostrando fino a che punto il 2012 spinge la specializzazione.","sLgbt":".lgbt fu delegata dal round 2012.","iLgbt":"Comunità e identità possono essere rappresentate direttamente al livello più alto del sistema dei nomi.","sBible":".bible fu delegata dal round 2012.","iBible":"Le comunità di fede si affiancano a brand, luoghi e industrie nel rivendicare uno spazio esplicito nella root.","sBeer":".beer fu delegata dal round 2012.","iBeer":"I prodotti non sono più soltanto cose vendute online: diventano categorie di lifestyle con namespace propri.","sPizza":".pizza fu delegata dal round 2012.","iPizza":"Il consumo quotidiano entra nel vocabolario del DNS, dai settori e brand fino alla cultura alimentare.","sWtf":".wtf fu delegata dal round 2012.","iWtf":"Lo slang di Internet diventa infrastruttura: forse la prova più sintetica che lo spazio dei nomi è ormai pienamente culturale.","sFail":".fail fu delegata dal round 2012.","iFail":"Perfino il fallimento diventa una categoria semantica praticabile nella root DNS. Siamo molto lontani dai soli nomi istituzionali.","pf2026":"Candidature pagate che ICANN ha confermato come procedenti nel round 2026.","pf2012apps":"Candidature ricevute nel round dei nuovi gTLD del 2012.","pfGeo":"Candidature 2012 identificate come geografiche nelle statistiche ICANN.","pfYears":"Anni trascorsi tra il round 2012 e il nuovo round di candidature 2026.","pfBerlin":".berlin è diventato nel 2014 il primo city-TLD ad aprire le registrazioni al pubblico.","plan1":"Importare il dataset APS ufficiale.","plan2":"Normalizzare stringhe primarie, sostitutive e varianti.","plan3":"Costruire i contention set e i conteggi per candidato.","plan4":"Classificare geografia/città, brand, community, AI/agent, identity/trust, finanza/crypto e altri temi editoriali.","plan5":"Confrontare con 2000, 2004 e 2012.","plan6":"Generare dati pronti per LinkedIn ed esportazioni scaricabili.","guard1":"“Geographic” è una classificazione ICANN delle candidature; “city TLD” è una classificazione analitica più stretta usata in questa pubblicazione.","guard2":"Numero di candidature, stringhe uniche, portafogli di registry, TLD gestiti come RSP e TLD delegati sono metriche diverse e non vengono mai confuse.","guard3":"Il dataset 2026 è ricavato da ICANN APS. Tra Reveal Day e String Confirmation Day, stringhe e contention set possono ancora cambiare attraverso il replacement process.","guard4":"I temi “zeitgeist” e il modello dei cinque livelli di potere sono classificazioni analitiche e restano separati dai campi ufficiali ICANN.","tabBeyond":"Oltre il dominio","tabGeo":"Geografia","tabLugano":".lugano","beyondTitle":"Quando un TLD diventa infrastruttura","beyondSub":"Alcuni namespace sono andati molto oltre il nome. Hanno aggiunto criteri di accesso, verifica, requisiti di sicurezza, governance d’interesse pubblico o servizi digitali condivisi.","maturityTitle":"Da un nome a uno strato di fiducia","maturitySub":"Il passaggio interessante non è quanti domini esistono, ma quanta informazione, responsabilità e verifica può incorporare il namespace.","m1":"Nome","m1b":"Un indirizzo riconoscibile.","m2":"Comunità qualificata","m2b":"Regole che definiscono chi può accedere.","m3":"Identità verificata","m3b":"I requisiti vengono controllati.","m4":"Baseline di sicurezza","m4b":"I requisiti tecnici diventano parte del namespace.","m5":"Servizi condivisi","m5b":"Il TLD diventa piattaforma per servizi comuni.","stageShared":"Servizi condivisi","stageCivic":"Infrastruttura civica","stageVerified":"Fiducia verificata","stagePublicId":"Identità pubblica verificata","stageQualification":"Qualifica esterna","stageCommitment":"Profilo + impegno","stageQualified":"Namespace qualificato","casePostTitle":"Un TLD con uno strato di servizi","casePostBody":"L’UPU verifica i registranti, impone un quadro di cybersicurezza e gestisce trust.post, una Shared Services Platform per il settore postale e logistico.","caseCatTitle":"Un dominio che finanzia un bene comune digitale","caseCatBody":"Il registro .cat reinveste i ricavi in diritti digitali, strumenti e progetti per la comunità catalanofona e descrive la missione più ampia come infrastruttura civica.","caseBankTitle":"Regole di sicurezza incorporate nell’appartenenza","caseBankBody":"I domini .bank e .insurance attivi devono rispettare requisiti come DNSSEC, TLS e autenticazione email. Il suffisso è progettato come ambiente a fiducia più elevata.","caseGovTitle":"Il suffisso diventa una credenziale istituzionale","caseGovBody":"Solo organizzazioni governative statunitensi verificate possono registrare .gov. CISA controlla eleggibilità e identità dei richiedenti.","casePharmacyTitle":"Un dominio legato a un accreditamento","casePharmacyBody":"Per ottenere .pharmacy occorre dimostrare la conformità agli standard NABP e ricevere l’accreditamento. Il dominio è quindi agganciato a una qualifica esterna.","caseEcoTitle":"L’attivazione richiede profilo e impegno","caseEcoBody":"Una registrazione .eco resta sospesa finché il registrante non conferma l’eleggibilità, completa un Eco Profile e assume pubblicamente un impegno ambientale.","caseSwissTitle":"Un namespace riservato a una comunità definita","caseSwissBody":".swiss è riservato alla comunità svizzera e applica requisiti che collegano registrante e denominazione richiesta alla Svizzera.","sourceCase":"Fonte primaria","geoTitle":"La geografia del potere nel DNS","geoSub":"Internet è fisicamente distribuita. Nessuna singola mappa mostra chi ‘controlla Internet’. Definizione delle regole, coordinamento della root, contratti di registro, capacità tecnica e domanda degli applicant si concentrano in luoghi diversi; questa vista mantiene separati i livelli.","geoPlaces":"Territori rappresentati","geoGovernance":"Coordinamento & root","geoRsp":"Offerta RSP valutata · 2026","geoPlacesNote":"Selezione ragionata di city e geographic TLD. Questo layer mostra cosa rappresenta il namespace: non è un censimento completo e non indica dove si trova il backend tecnico.","geoGovernanceNote":"Sedi selezionate di ICANN, PTI/IANA e Root Zone Maintainer. Le policy ICANN nascono da un processo multistakeholder globale; PTI valida le modifiche della root e Verisign le implementa. Le sedi non indicano dove viene fisicamente erogato il servizio della root né dove transita Internet.","geoRspNote":"Organizzazioni RSP valutate per regione ICANN al 31 agosto 2026: Europa 16, Asia-Pacifico 7, Nord America 6, Africa 0, America Latina e Caraibi 0. È la geografia dell’offerta tecnica pre-valutata. Gli RSP scelti dagli applicant non sono pubblicati nei dati delle candidature 2026 (domanda 176), quindi non è una quota di mercato degli applicant.","registryGeoTitle":"Dove “risiedono” i TLD?","registryGeoBody":"La risposta utile ha tre livelli: il luogo rappresentato dal TLD, il domicilio giuridico del Registry Operator e il backend tecnico distribuito. IANA registra il manager di ogni delega. L’aggregazione completa per Paese dei manager sarà costruita con il dataset 2026, senza dedurla da marchi o localizzazione dei server.","powerTitle":"Dove si concentra il potere del DNS?","powerBody":"Politica, gestione della root zone, manutenzione, operazioni di registro e backend tecnico sono ruoli diversi. Mappandoli separatamente si può vedere la concentrazione senza fingere che un singolo soggetto “possieda Internet”.","rootStat1":"12 operatori indipendenti dei root server","rootStat2":"13 identità logiche dei root server (A–M)","rootStat3":"2.045 istanze operative dei root server al 30 settembre 2026","rspStat":"29 organizzazioni RSP valutate ad agosto 2026","mapHint":"Seleziona un livello della mappa. Passa il mouse o tocca un punto per il contesto.","nodeLA":"Sede centrale ICANN e Public Technical Identifiers (funzioni IANA).","nodeReston":"Sede Verisign; Verisign svolge il ruolo di Root Zone Maintainer.","nodeRegional":"Ufficio regionale ICANN.","nodeEngagement":"Engagement center ICANN.","luganoTitle":".lugano — da un nome a uno strato di fiducia","luganoSub":"Una visione di ciò che il namespace potrebbe diventare se la candidatura avrà successo. È una direzione, non l’annuncio di servizi già realizzati.","visionBadge":"VISIONE · NON ANCORA IMPLEMENTATA","luganoLead":"L’ambizione non è semplicemente mettere Lugano dopo un punto. È creare un namespace d’interesse pubblico nel quale la fiducia possa diventare riconoscibile, governabile e, nel tempo, leggibile anche dalle macchine.","lugPillar1":"Responsabilità pubblica","lugPillar1b":"Regole, nomi riservati e scelte strategiche restano sotto responsabilità pubblica.","lugPillar2":"Partecipazione qualificata","lugPillar2b":"Categorie diverse di utenti o servizi possono avere criteri espliciti di accesso e responsabilità.","lugPillar3":"Sicurezza e interoperabilità","lugPillar3b":"Requisiti tecnici minimi e interfacce aperte possono trasformare la fiducia da simbolica a operativa.","lugPillar4":"Fiducia leggibile dalle macchine","lugPillar4b":"Nel lungo periodo software e agenti AI potrebbero verificare con chi interagiscono, cosa un servizio è autorizzato a fare e quali regole si applicano.","lugRoadmap":"Una progressione possibile","lugRoadmapSub":"Ogni passaggio richiederebbe proprie decisioni di governance, giuridiche e tecniche dopo un eventuale esito positivo del processo ICANN.","lugStage1":"Fondazione sicura del registro","lugStage1b":"Politiche del registro, nomi riservati, domini istituzionali, gestione degli abusi e continuità operativa.","lugStage2":"Ecosistema verificato","lugStage2b":"Organizzazioni, servizi e attori locali qualificati con condizioni d’uso chiare e responsabilità trasparenti.","lugStage3":"Strato di bene comune digitale","lugStage3b":"Componenti tecniche condivise, metadati e servizi interoperabili che riducano l’attrito per l’ecosistema locale.","lugStage4":"Fiducia pronta per gli agenti","lugStage4b":"Identità, delega e discovery leggibili dalle macchine, così che anche i sistemi intelligenti possano valutare la fiducia.","luganoGuard":"Nulla in questa sezione implica che ICANN abbia assegnato .lugano o che questi servizi esistano già. Descrive una possibile direzione progettuale, subordinata all’esito della candidatura e alle future decisioni della Città.","geoSourceLine":"Metodo: policy, coordinamento della root, contratti, backend tecnico e domanda sono livelli distinti. La concentrazione geografica mostra un’asimmetria, non dimostra che un luogo o una società “controlli Internet”.","beyondQuote":"Alcuni dei casi più chiari mostrano che un TLD può incorporare molto più di un nome: eleggibilità, identità, sicurezza, regole di comunità e servizi condivisi.","eyebrow":"ICANN · DNS · CITTÀ · SOCIETÀ","fact":"FATTO","reading":"LETTURA","vision":"VISIONE","legendText":"informazione verificata · interpretazione dei dati · scenario futuro","delegationRecords":"delegazioni registrate · round 2012","delegationCaveat":"ICANN riporta 1.241 delegazioni dal round 2012; il totale non è rettificato per TLD successivamente terminati o rimossi dalla root zone.","source2012":"Statistiche ICANN 2012","sourceReveal":"ICANN Reveal Day","mobileNavigate":"Naviga","roundAtGlance":"I round a colpo d’occhio","roundAtGlanceSub":"Quattro aperture, scale radicalmente diverse. I valori indicano le candidature, non i TLD poi effettivamente delegati.","applicantRegion2012":"Domanda candidati · 2012","applicantRegion2012Note":"Distribuzione regionale ufficiale ICANN delle 1.930 candidature, basata sulla sede principale dichiarata dal candidato. Non indica dove un TLD “risiede”.","rspEvaluated":"RSP valutati","knownReveal":"Cosa sapremo al Reveal Day","unknownReveal":"Cosa non sapremo ancora","knownRevealSub":"Alle 18:00 UTC del 7 ottobre ICANN pubblicherà i dati pubblici delle candidature.","unknownRevealSub":"Il reveal apre la valutazione: non equivale all’assegnazione di un TLD.","known1":"Le stringhe primarie richieste e i candidati che le hanno presentate.","known2":"Le eventuali stringhe variant o replacement.","known3":"I contention set iniziali.","known4":"Le parti pubbliche di ciascuna candidatura.","unknown1":"Quali candidature supereranno tutte le valutazioni.","unknown2":"Chi prevarrà nei contention set non risolti.","unknown3":"Quando un TLD riuscito sarà delegato o entrerà in funzione.","unknown4":"Che .lugano sia stato assegnato: resterà una candidatura finché il processo non sarà completato.","revealLocal":"Reveal Day: 7 ottobre 2026 · 18:00 UTC · 20:00 a Lugano","curatedSample":"selezione ragionata","downloadExcel":"Workbook Excel completo","excelSub":"XLSX · tabelle pronte all’uso + fonti","dataPackSub":"ZIP · workbook Excel + tutti i dataset CSV","footerLeftV05":"Snapshot pre-Reveal · aggiornato 2 ottobre 2026 · 1984–2026","footerRightV05":"Fonti primarie: ICANN, IANA e fonti registry / governance citate","roleTagPolicy":"POLICY","roleTagRoot":"ROOT","roleTagGovernance":"GOVERNANCE","roleTagTech":"TECNICA","roleTagRetail":"REGISTRAZIONE","roleTagUser":"UTENTE","searchLabel":"Cerca in Strange Internet","roundFilterLabel":"Round","categoryFilterLabel":"Filtra per categoria","mapLabel":"Layer della mappa","methodLegendTitle":"Come leggere questa pubblicazione","methodLegendBody":"FATTO indica un’informazione verificabile sostenuta da una fonte citata. LETTURA indica una nostra interpretazione dei dati o del quadro storico. VISIONE indica uno scenario, una direzione o una proposta futura. Lo stato del progetto è indicato separatamente, perché stato e natura dell’affermazione non sono la stessa cosa.","downloadGeoRegions":"Geografia candidati 2012","downloadGeoRegionsSub":"CSV · totali regionali ufficiali ICANN","beyondDownload":"Oltre il punto","rspDownload":"Geografia RSP","secondarySource":"FONTE SECONDARIA","statusApplication2026":"CANDIDATURA 2026","statusNotImplemented":"NON ANCORA IMPLEMENTATO","luganoStatusTitle":"Stato attuale","luganoStatusBody":"La Città di Lugano ha presentato la candidatura per .lugano nel round ICANN 2026. Una candidatura non equivale ad assegnazione né a delega: .lugano diventa attivo solo dopo le valutazioni previste, la contrattualizzazione e la delega nella root zone.","operator":"Fonte operatore","awaitReveal":"DATI UFFICIALI 2026 · DA INSERIRE","awaitRevealShort":"Dati ufficiali 2026 da inserire","afterReveal":"ANALISI 2026","provisionalUntilConfirmation":"PROVVISORIO FINO AL 17 NOV","homeUnique2026":"stringhe primarie uniche · 2026","homeApplicants2026":"organizzazioni candidate · 2026","homeContention2026":"contention set iniziali · 2026","homeBenchmark2012":"Benchmark 2012: 1.930 candidature · ≈1.400 stringhe uniche · 230 contention set per corrispondenza esatta · 66 candidature geografiche.","round2026Title":"Il round 2026","round2026Sub":"Prima del Reveal Day mostriamo come fatti soltanto i dati già confermati. Gli altri campi 2026 sono riservati al dataset ufficiale ICANN che sarà pubblicato il 7 ottobre.","round2026Snapshot":"Dati confermati prima del Reveal","round2026Unique":"stringhe primarie uniche","round2026Applicants":"organizzazioni candidate","round2026Contention":"contention set iniziali","round2026Geo":"candidature geografiche","round2026Idn":"candidature IDN / variant","dataStatusTitle":"Il Reveal Day sarà uno snapshot, non l’elenco definitivo delle stringhe","dataStatusSub":"ICANN pubblicherà il dataset iniziale il 7 ottobre. Gli applicant ammessi potranno passare a una replacement string dall’8 al 21 ottobre; ICANN prevede di pubblicare l’elenco confermato delle stringhe e i contention set aggiornati il 17 novembre.","milestoneReveal":"7 OTT · REVEAL DAY","milestoneRevealBody":"Candidati, stringhe primarie, eventuali variant/replacement, contention set iniziali e parti pubbliche delle candidature.","milestoneReplacement":"8–21 OTT · REPLACEMENT PERIOD","milestoneReplacementBody":"I candidati idonei possono sostituire la stringa primaria con una replacement string.","milestoneConfirm":"17 NOV · CONFERMA DELLA STRINGA","milestoneConfirmBody":"Elenco finale delle stringhe richieste e contention set aggiornati.","round2026Patterns":"Cosa ci può raccontare il 2026","round2026PatternsSub":"Lo stesso dataset ufficiale può essere letto da più angolazioni, senza ridurlo a una semplice classifica.","patternGeo":"Da dove arriva la domanda","patternGeoBody":"Regioni, paesi e concentrazione delle organizzazioni applicant, ricavati dalla loro sede operativa pubblica.","patternApplicants":"Chi presenta le candidature","patternApplicantsBody":"Applicant legali, gruppi societari, candidati ricorrenti, brand, community, geografie e strategie di portafoglio.","patternWords":"Come vuole chiamarsi il nuovo Internet","patternWordsBody":"Cluster semantici, stringhe insolite e vocabolario sociale o tecnologico del 2026.","patternCompetition":"Dove resta la scarsità","patternCompetitionBody":"Contention set, stringhe ripetute e aree nelle quali molti candidati hanno convergito sulla stessa idea.","geoLayer2026":"Livello geografico · 2026","geoApps2026":"candidature geografiche nel round 2026","geoApps2026Note":"Dopo il Reveal Day, il valore ufficiale sarà ricavato dal dataset ICANN APS.","geoCities2026":"stringhe cittadine / territoriali nel 2026","geoCities2026Note":"Classificazione ricavata dai dati pubblici delle candidature 2026.","powerStackTitle":"Il potere è una stratificazione, non un punto sulla mappa","powerStackSub":"Contano cinque livelli e descrivono fenomeni diversi. Fonderli in un unico “centro di Internet” sarebbe semplice, suggestivo e sbagliato.","powerRules":"Regole","powerRulesBody":"Il processo multistakeholder globale di ICANN sviluppa le policy; ICANN org attua il quadro contrattuale e operativo del programma gTLD.","powerRoot":"Coordinamento della root","powerRootBody":"PTI valida le richieste di modifica della root zone; Verisign implementa le modifiche validate come Root Zone Maintainer. Il servizio della root è invece distribuito globalmente tra operatori indipendenti.","powerContracts":"Contratti di registro","powerContractsBody":"Ogni registry operator assume la responsabilità contrattuale del proprio namespace. Alcuni operatori detengono portafogli molto ampi di Registry Agreement.","powerBackend":"Backend tecnico","powerBackendBody":"I Registry Service Provider forniscono infrastruttura di registro, DNS e DNSSEC a molti TLD. La concentrazione tecnica può essere molto diversa dalla proprietà dei namespace.","powerDemand":"Domanda","powerDemandBody":"Gli applicant decidono quali stringhe vale la pena richiedere. Le loro sedi operative pubbliche mostrano da dove nasce la domanda commerciale, civica e istituzionale.","geoMapTitle":"Una mappa, quattro domande diverse","geoMapSub":"Cambia livello per vedere coordinamento istituzionale, offerta tecnica valutata e domanda degli applicant. Si sovrappongono, ma non rappresentano lo stesso tipo di potere.","applicantRegion2026":"Domanda candidati · 2026","applicantRegion2026Note":"Dopo il Reveal Day, questa distribuzione sarà ricavata dalle sedi principali pubbliche degli applicant nel dataset ufficiale 2026. Mapperà le organizzazioni candidate, non gli utenti, l’infrastruttura DNS o la geografia dell’attività economica finale.","demand2012Title":"Domanda dei candidati · 2012","demand2012Sub":"ICANN registrò 1.930 candidature da 60 Paesi e territori. Nord America ed Europa insieme rappresentavano l’82,2%.","demand2012Concentration":"82,2% · Nord America + Europa","rspSupplyTitle":"Offerta tecnica · RSP valutati","rspSupplySub":"Ad agosto 2026, 29 organizzazioni avevano superato almeno una valutazione RSP. L’Europa ne contava 16, pari al 55,2%.","rspEuropeShare":"55,2% · Europa","demand2026Title":"Domanda dei candidati · 2026","demand2026Sub":"Dopo il Reveal Day questa vista sarà ricavata dalle sedi principali pubbliche degli applicant nel dataset ufficiale 2026. Descriverà la geografia della domanda, non quella dell’infrastruttura.","topCountries2026Title":"Principali Paesi dei candidati · 2026","topCountries2026Sub":"Dopo il Reveal Day questa classifica sarà ricavata dalle sedi principali pubbliche degli applicant nel dataset ufficiale 2026.","identityTitle":"Chi ha costruito il portafoglio TLD più ampio? Donuts → Identity Digital","identitySub":"Donuts fu di gran lunga il principale candidato nel 2012, con 307 candidature presentate tramite società controllate. L’attività è poi cresciuta attraverso acquisizioni e oggi opera come Identity Digital, che ICANN descrive come il più grande operatore di registri TLD al mondo.","identityApps":"candidature presentate da Donuts nel 2012","identityPortfolio":"TLD nel portafoglio attuale di Identity Digital","identityManaged":"TLD che Identity Digital dichiara di gestire tecnicamente","identityHQ":"sede principale · Bellevue, Washington","identityPortfolioClaim":"Identity Digital descrive un portafoglio di quasi 300 TLD. Dichiara inoltre di gestire tecnicamente oltre 460 gTLD, ccTLD e dotBrand come Registry Services Provider. Sono misure diverse: portafoglio / gestione del namespace non equivale alla gestione tecnica del backend.","identityT2012":"2012 · 307 candidature","identityT2017":"2017 · acquisizione Rightside","identityT2020":"2020 · acquisizione Afilias","identityT2022":"2022 · nasce il brand Identity Digital","identityT2026":"2026 · ~300 TLD in portafoglio / 460+ gestiti","identityReadingTitle":"Perché emerge la concentrazione?","identityReadingBody":"La gestione di un registry comporta costi fissi rilevanti per infrastruttura, sicurezza, compliance e distribuzione tramite registrar. Un portafoglio ampio consente di riutilizzare backend e canali commerciali, distribuire il rischio tra molte stringhe di nicchia e crescere ulteriormente tramite acquisizioni. Donuts indicava esplicitamente le economie di scala come ragione centrale della strategia 2012.","identityCaveat":"Candidature ≠ TLD ottenuti ≠ Registry Agreement ≠ portafoglio TLD ≠ TLD gestiti come RSP ≠ governance di Internet. I numeri appartengono a livelli diversi e non vanno sommati.","identity2026Top":"Maggiore applicant legale · 2026","identity2026TopSub":"Dopo il Reveal Day, questo dato sarà calcolato sul dataset ufficiale 2026. Gli applicant legali saranno conteggiati come pubblicati; una vista separata potrà raggruppare le candidature per società madre diretta / ultima quando i dati pubblici lo consentiranno.","rootDistributedTitle":"La root è coordinata da poche istituzioni, ma servita globalmente","rootDistributedBody":"PTI gestisce le modifiche della root zone e Verisign mantiene il file della root, mentre 12 operatori indipendenti servono 13 identità logiche della root attraverso migliaia di istanze fisiche nel mondo. Coordinamento istituzionale e distribuzione fisica hanno quindi geografie molto diverse.","geoInterpretTitle":"Che cosa intendiamo qui per “potere di Internet”","geoInterpretBody":"La dashboard può mostrare dove si concentrano istituzioni di policy, funzioni della root, contratti di registro, sedi degli applicant e fornitori tecnici. Non può ridurre la governance di Internet a una sede, né equiparare un grande portafoglio TLD al controllo delle policy ICANN o della root DNS.","contentionTitle2026":"Dove i candidati si scontrano nel 2026","contention2026Sub":"I contention set iniziali saranno disponibili al Reveal Day. Potranno ancora cambiare per replacement string e successive procedure di string confusion; il 17 novembre offrirà un punto di confronto più pulito.","contention2026Label":"contention set iniziali · 2026","contention2012Title":"Il benchmark 2012","contentionCompareTitle":"La competizione è anche un segnale culturale","contentionCompareBody":"Le stringhe più contese mostrano dove molti candidati hanno immaginato indipendentemente lo stesso mercato futuro. Confrontare 2012 e 2026 trasforma la contention da dettaglio procedurale a misura del cambiamento delle aspettative.","downloadRound2026":"Dati round 2026 (CSV)","downloadRound2026Sub":"CSV · metriche ufficiali 2026, metodo e fonti","downloadConcentration":"Concentrazione registry","downloadConcentrationSub":"CSV · cronologia Donuts / Identity Digital, metriche e fonti","downloadGeo2026":"Geografia candidati 2026","downloadGeo2026Sub":"CSV · geografia degli applicant ricavata dai dati pubblici 2026","homeCountries2026":"paesi e territori rappresentati · 2026","round2026Countries":"paesi e territori rappresentati","round2026Community":"candidature community","round2026Brand":"candidature .Brand","identityGap":"Nel 2012 Donuts presentò 307 candidature, oltre tre volte le 101 di Google, il secondo applicant; Amazon ne presentò 78.","identity2026TopGroup":"Maggiore gruppo societario · 2026","identity2026GroupSub":"Dopo il Reveal Day, la normalizzazione per gruppo societario userà soltanto le informazioni pubbliche sulle società madri nelle candidature ICANN e resterà distinta dalla classifica degli applicant legali.","downloadRegions2026":"Geografia applicant 2026 (CSV)","downloadRegistryConcentration":"Concentrazione registry (CSV)","scaleContrastTitle":"Due forme diverse di concentrazione","scaleContrastSub":"Contare i TLD e contare i domini registrati sotto quei TLD produce due immagini molto diverse della scala.","identityBreadthLabel":"TLD nel portafoglio Identity Digital · ampiezza dei namespace","verisignDepthLabel":"registrazioni .com + .net · Q2 2026","verisignInfraLabel":"Root Zone Maintainer · operatore di A-root e J-root","scaleContrastReading":"Non esiste una classifica unica del «potere di Internet». Identity Digital concentra l’ampiezza del portafoglio su molti TLD; Verisign combina una scala eccezionale di registrazioni sotto .com/.net con importanti ruoli operativi sulla root. Nessuno dei due indicatori equivale al controllo delle politiche ICANN.","applications2026Download":"Dati candidatura per candidatura · 2026 (CSV)","applications2026DownloadSub":"CSV · schema normalizzato per il dataset pubblico ufficiale delle candidature 2026","strange2026Pending":"I dati delle candidature 2026 sono già previsti nella struttura della pubblicazione. Questa versione di lavoro attende il dataset ufficiale ICANN; la versione post-Reveal popolerà questo livello con stringhe e fonti reali.","tabExplore":"Esplora","exploreTitle":"Namespace Explorer · anteprima","exploreSub":"Una ricerca ragionata tra TLD attivi selezionati, candidature storiche e il dataset 2026 in arrivo. L’assenza da questa anteprima non significa che una stringa non esista.","exploreCoverageTitle":"Corpus in anteprima","exploreCoverageBody":"Questa build indicizza un corpus iniziale ragionato mentre prepariamo l’importazione dell’intera root IANA e del dataset ufficiale delle candidature 2026. La copertura è volutamente incompleta e dichiarata come tale.","exploreIana":"TLD attivi nello snapshot della root IANA","exploreSeed":"record indicizzati in questa build","exploreSearchPlaceholder":"Cerca un TLD, un applicant, un luogo o un tema…","exploreSearchHint":"La ricerca usa nomi e tag editoriali: “fiducia”, “città” o “identità” possono quindi trovare record collegati.","exploreRound":"Round","exploreStatus":"Stato","exploreType":"Tipo","exploreTheme":"Tema","allStatuses":"Tutti gli stati","allTypes":"Tutti i tipi","allThemes":"Tutti i temi","presetAll":"Tutti","preset2026":"2026","presetStrange":"Strani","presetCities":"Città e luoghi","presetContention":"Contesi","presetTrust":"Fiducia & identità","exploreResults":"record","exploreNoResults":"Nessun record corrispondente.","exploreCorpusNote":"Nota sulla copertura","exploreCorpusNoteBody":"Questa è un’anteprima ragionata, non un indice completo del namespace. Dopo il Reveal Day importeremo il dataset ufficiale 2026 e amplieremo la copertura senza cambiare l’interfaccia.","exploreColTld":"TLD / stringa","exploreColStatus":"Stato","exploreColRound":"Round","exploreColEntity":"Applicant / gestore","exploreColGeo":"Geografia","exploreColType":"Tipo","exploreColTheme":"Tema","exploreOpen":"Apri scheda","exploreFactFields":"Campi verificati","exploreReadingFields":"Lettura editoriale","exploreSources":"Fonti","exploreClose":"Chiudi","exploreApplicantManager":"Applicant / gestore","exploreGroup":"Gruppo societario","exploreGeography":"Geografia","exploreContention":"Candidature in contesa","exploreContext":"Contesto","exploreReading":"Interpretazione","typeApplication":"Candidatura","typeGeneric":"Generico","typeSponsored":"Sponsorizzato / ristretto","typeInfrastructure":"Infrastrutturale","typeGeographic":"Geografico","typeBrand":"Brand","typeCommunity":"Community","typeCountryCode":"Codice paese","legacy":"Legacy","application":"Candidatura","business":"Business","technology":"Tecnologia","education":"Istruzione","government":"Governo","infrastructure":"Infrastruttura","ai":"AI & agenti","preset":"Vista","strangeExplorerCta":"Esplora questi casi e l’intero namespace","strangeExplorerNote":"Internet strano diventa una lente editoriale. Ricerca e filtri stanno nell’Explorer, dove questi record convivono con il resto dello spazio dei nomi.","searchLabelExplorer":"Cerca nello spazio dei nomi","statusFilterLabel":"Filtra per stato","typeFilterLabel":"Filtra per tipo","themeFilterLabel":"Filtra per tema","explorePlaceholderBadge":"DATASET PROVVISORIO","exploreIanaDate":"Snapshot IANA · 2 ott 2026","projectByline":"Progetto di ricerca indipendente di <strong>Robert Bregy</strong>.","projectDisclaimer":"Il progetto non rappresenta una posizione ufficiale della Città di Lugano.","footerAttribution":"Ricerca, analisi e interpretazione editoriale di Robert Bregy.","navThemes":"Temi","navRound":"Round 2026","navResearch":"Ricerca","publicationState":"SNAPSHOT PRE-REVEAL · 2 OTT 2026","publicationStateNote":"Reveal Day · 7 ott 2026 · 18:00 UTC","exploreLoadMore":"Carica altri","exploreShowing":"visualizzati {shown} di {total}","roundAwaitingTitle":"Disponibile al Reveal Day · 7 ottobre 2026","roundAwaitingBody":"Il dataset ufficiale popolerà stringhe uniche, organizzazioni candidate, paesi e territori, contention set, candidature geografiche/community/.Brand e campi IDN/variant.","luganoVisionCondition":"VISIONE · IN CASO DI DELEGAZIONE","shareLabel":"Condividi questa vista","themeLabel":"Cambia tema","languageLabel":"Lingua","closeLabel":"Chiudi la scheda Explorer","drawerLabel":"Scheda Explorer","mapItemLabel":"Elemento mappa: {name}","resourcesNav":"Risorse","methodNav":"Metodo e fonti","metaDescription":"Da .arpa a .lugano: come i domini Internet sono diventati identità, infrastruttura e fiducia. Una pubblicazione di ricerca indipendente di Robert Bregy.","metaOgDescription":"Ciò che mettiamo dopo il punto dice qualcosa sul mondo che immaginiamo.","tocLabel":"In questa sezione","homeLabel":"Vai a Panoramica","sectionNavLabel":"Sezioni","explorerPresetsLabel":"Filtri rapidi Explorer","timelineStageProtocol":"PROTOCOLLO","timelineStageRoot":"TAPPA DELLA ROOT","timelineStageIdea":"IDEA","timelineStageRound":"ROUND DI CANDIDATURA","timelineStageLaunch":"DELEGAZIONE / LANCIO"},"de":{"brand":"Marke","copyLink":"Link kopieren","copied":"Kopiert","heroTitle":"Connecting the Dots","heroBody":"Von .arpa bis .lugano: wie Internet-Domains zu Identität, Infrastruktur und Vertrauen wurden.","tabOverview":"Überblick","tabArchaeology":"Archäologie","tabHow":"So funktioniert es","tabCities":"Städte & Regionen","tabContention":"Konkurrenz","tabStrange":"Seltsames Internet","tabReveal":"Runde 2026","tabPress":"Daten & Quellen","tabSources":"Methodik","applicationsRound":"Bewerbungen pro Runde","applicationsRoundSub":"Der Sprung von 2012 veränderte die Größenordnung des Namensraums.","icann2012":"2012: Was ICANN erhielt","icann2012Sub":"Dies sind getrennte offizielle Klassifikationen, keine Anteile eines Ganzen.","overviewCallout":"Was wir hinter den Punkt setzen, sagt etwas über die Welt, die wir uns vorstellen.","overviewFoot":"Offizielle ICANN-Felder bleiben von der redaktionellen Interpretation getrennt. Geschichte soll lesbar werden, ohne unbemerkt zu Marketing zu werden.","archTitle":"Eine kurze Archäologie der Expansion","archSub":"Einige Meilensteine sind ICANN-Runden, andere erklären, wie Stadt-TLDs möglich wurden.","timeCapsule":"Das Jahr 2000 wirkt bereits wie eine Zeitkapsel","timeCapsuleSub":"Eine kuratierte Auswahl aus der Proof-of-Concept-Runde.","string":"String","theme":"Thema","status":"Status","why":"Warum es zählt","scopeGeneral":"Allgemeine Prinzipien","scopeGeneralSub":"Wie DNS, TLD-Rollen und das Verfahren für neue gTLDs unabhängig von .lugano funktionieren.","scopeLugano":".lugano · Anwendungsfall","scopeLuganoSub":"Was diese allgemeinen Regeln für Luganos Bewerbung in der Runde 2026 bedeuten.","luganoAppliedTitle":"Von der allgemeinen Regel zum Fall .lugano","luganoAppliedBody":"Die Bewerbung für .lugano durchläuft denselben oben beschriebenen Prozess. Bis Bewertung, Vertrag und Delegation abgeschlossen sind, bleibt .lugano eine Bewerbung und ist keine aktive TLD.","luganoExampleLabel":"Hypothetisches Beispiel","luganoExampleBody":"Wird .lugano delegiert, kann dieselbe DNS-Struktur Adressen wie servizi.lugano oder www.servizi.lugano ermöglichen. Das Beispiel zeigt die Hierarchie; es bezeichnet keinen bereits aktiven Dienst.","luganoLanguageGuide":"Präzise über .lugano sprechen","luganoLanguageGuideSub":"Zwei wichtige Unterscheidungen, solange das Bewerbungsverfahren noch läuft.","howTitle":"Wie Internet-Domains wirklich funktionieren","howSub":"Die wesentlichen Begriffe, um sich zwischen Domains, DNS und Internet-Governance zu orientieren.","anatomyTitle":"Beginnen wir mit einer Adresse","anatomyBody":"In <b>www.example.com</b> ist <b>.com</b> die Top-Level-Domain (TLD). „example“ ist der direkt darunter registrierte Name. „www“ ist ein optionales Label vor diesem registrierten Namen.","rootLabel":"Root","rootBody":"Die normalerweise unsichtbare oberste Ebene der DNS-Hierarchie.","tldLabel":"Top-Level-Domain","tldBody":"Der letzte Teil: .ch, .com, .org.","secondLabel":"Registrierter Name","secondBody":"Der Name direkt links von der TLD, zum Beispiel example.com.","hostLabel":"Optionale Bezeichnung","hostBody":"Ein optionales Label vor dem registrierten Namen. In www.example.com kann „www“ eine bestimmte Website oder einen Dienst bezeichnen; es ist nicht Teil der TLD.","whoTitle":"Wer macht was?","whoSub":"Das Internet hat keinen einzigen Eigentümer. Es funktioniert durch getrennte Rollen, Verträge und technische Koordination.","roleIcann":"ICANN","roleIcannBody":"Koordiniert Regeln und Verträge für generische Top-Level-Domains in einem globalen Multistakeholder-Modell.","roleIana":"IANA / PTI","roleIanaBody":"Führt die autoritative Root-Zone-Datenbank und koordiniert technisch die Delegation von TLDs in die DNS-Root.","roleRegistry":"Registry-Betreiber","roleRegistryBody":"Betreibt die Regeln und die autoritative Datenbank einer TLD. Der Registry Operator ist für diesen Namensraum innerhalb des ICANN-Vertragsrahmens verantwortlich.","roleRsp":"Registry Service Provider","roleRspBody":"Liefert kritische technische Registry-Dienste wie DNS- und Registrierungsinfrastruktur.","roleRegistrar":"Registrar / Registrierungsstelle","roleRegistrarBody":"Die Vermittlungs-/Vertriebsebene, über die Domainnamen üblicherweise registriert und verwaltet werden.","roleRegistrant":"Domaininhaber (Registrant)","roleRegistrantBody":"Person oder Organisation, die einen Domainnamen unter der TLD registriert und nutzt.","birthTitle":"Wie eine neue TLD entsteht","birthSub":"2026 gilt nicht “beantragen und erhalten”, sondern ein öffentliches, mehrstufiges Verfahren.","step1":"Antrag","step1b":"Der Antragsteller reicht die Unterlagen ein und zahlt die Gebühr.","step2":"Reveal","step2b":"ICANN veröffentlicht öffentliche Antragsdaten und identifiziert identische Strings in Konkurrenz.","step3":"Input & Einsprachen","step3b":"Community-Input, formelle Einsprachen und relevante Regierungsberatung können Teil des Verfahrens werden.","step4":"Prüfungen","step4b":"String, Antragsteller sowie operative, finanzielle und technische Komponenten werden geprüft.","step5":"Konkurrenz","step5b":"Bei identischen oder verwechselbaren Strings muss die Konkurrenz gelöst werden.","step6":"Vertrag","step6b":"Ein erfolgreicher Antragsteller unterzeichnet den Base Registry Agreement mit ICANN.","step7":"Delegation","step7b":"Nach Onboarding und technischen Tests kann IANA/PTI die TLD in die DNS-Root eintragen.","step8":"Start","step8b":"Erst danach kann die Registry Namen unter der TLD tatsächlich einsetzen und vergeben.","controlTitle":"Im Fall .lugano: was die Registry regeln könnte und was dies nicht bedeuten würde","canTitle":"Was die Registry regeln könnte","can1":"Zulassungs- und Vergaberegeln für Namen unter .lugano, innerhalb der ICANN-Regeln und des anwendbaren Rechts.","can2":"Reservierte Namen, Launch-Phasen und lokale Governance-Entscheide.","can3":"Registrierungs-, technische und Sicherheitsanforderungen für Namen unter .lugano.","cannotTitle":"Was dies nicht bedeuten würde","cannot1":"Lugano 'besitzt das Internet' nicht und kontrolliert weder .ch, .com noch andere TLDs.","cannot2":"Eine Bewerbung ist keine Zuteilung: .lugano wird erst nach Verfahren, Vertrag und Delegation aktiv.","cannot3":"ICANN betreibt die Websites der Stadt nicht; es koordiniert das globale Namenssystem und den vertraglichen Rahmen für gTLDs.","sourceGuide":"Offizielle Erklärung","cityTitle":"Vom Ortsnamen zur öffentlichen Infrastruktur","citySub":"Die Idee der Stadt-TLD ist mehr als ein Jahrzehnt älter als die Runde 2012.","geoLayer":"Geografische Ebene 2012","geoApps":"als geografisch eingestufte Bewerbungen","geoDelegated":"aus der Runde 2012 delegierte geografische gTLDs laut ICANN-Statistik","secondGen":"Eine zweite Generation von Stadt-TLDs?","secondGenBody":"Die erste Welle drehte sich vor allem um Identität, Lokalität und Registrierung. Ein städtischer Namensraum 2026 kann zusätzlich auf vertrauenswürdige öffentliche Dienste, verifizierbare Identität, maschinenlesbare Governance und Interaktion mit KI-Agenten ausgerichtet werden. Das ist eine redaktionelle Hypothese, keine ICANN-Klassifikation.","contentionTitle":"Was 2012 alle wollten","contentionSub":"Konkurrenz um Strings ist ein erstaunlich gutes Abbild der App-, Plattform- und E-Commerce-Ära.","strangeTitle":"Das seltsame Internet","strangeSub":"Nicht nur schräge Namen. Kleine Fossilien dessen, was jede Epoche für wichtig genug hielt, um einen eigenen Teil des Internet zu verdienen.","searchPlaceholder":"Suche .wtf, mobile, Identität, Vertrauen…","allRounds":"Alle Runden","crossRound":"Rundenübergreifend","allCategories":"Alle Kategorien","reset":"Zurücksetzen","searchHint":"Suche nach einer TLD, einem gesellschaftlichen Thema oder einer Idee.","results":"Ergebnisse","noResults":"Keine Treffer. Selbst dem Internet gehen gelegentlich die Merkwürdigkeiten aus.","source":"Quelle","sourcesN":"Quellen","whatItWas":"Was es war","whatItSays":"Was es über die Epoche sagt","strangeNext":"<b>2026-Ebene:</b> Nach Reveal Day werden die offiziellen Bewerbungen mit derselben Disziplin wie das historische Material klassifiziert: Der Bewerbungsdatensatz ist ein FAKT; die kulturelle oder technologische Bedeutung, die wir daraus ableiten, ist eine EINORDNUNG.","official2026":"Offizieller Stand 2026","paidApps":"bezahlte Bewerbungen im Verfahren","revealBody":"ICANN veröffentlicht die öffentlichen Bewerbungsdaten am 7. Oktober 2026 um 18:00 UTC, einschließlich der primären Strings und identischer String-Konkurrenzen.","preRevealIntel":"Pre-Reveal-Informationen","disclosed":"freiwillig öffentlich gemachte Strings, die TLDz per 26. September 2026 erfasst hat","secondaryNote":"Sekundärquelle. Sie ist nicht die offizielle ICANN-Liste und wird durch die Reveal-Day-Daten ersetzt.","importPlan":"Importplan für den Reveal Day","pressTitle":"Daten, Fakten & wiederverwendbare Ressourcen","pressSub":"Geprüfte Fakten, Definitionen und wiederverwendbare Ressourcen, um die Daten korrekt einzuordnen, ohne sie zu überinterpretieren.","safeFacts":"Fünf Schlüsselfakten","copyFact":"Kopieren","languageGuide":"Präzise Begriffe, klare Konzepte","languageGuideSub":"Die wesentlichen Begriffe, um sich zwischen Domains, DNS und Internet-Governance zu orientieren.","sayThis":"Präziser","notThis":"Nicht dasselbe wie","safe1":"Die Stadt Lugano hat sich um die neue Top-Level-Domain .lugano beworben.","avoid1":"Lugano hat die Domain .lugano erhalten / besitzt sie.","safe2":"Eine Top-Level-Domain ist der letzte Teil einer Internetadresse, zum Beispiel .ch oder .com.","avoid2":"Eine TLD ist einfach eine neue Website-Adresse.","safe3":"ICANN koordiniert das globale Domain-Namensystem und das Verfahren für neue gTLDs.","avoid3":"ICANN ist die Regierung oder der Eigentümer des Internets.","safe4":"Bei Erfolg könnte .lugano zu einem geregelten Namensraum werden, dessen Registry Operator innerhalb des ICANN-Rahmens Regeln festlegt.","avoid4":"Jeder wird automatisch jeden beliebigen .lugano-Namen registrieren können.","downloadsTitle":"Arbeitsdaten herunterladen","downloadsSub":"Die zugrunde liegenden Datensätze können zur Überprüfung, Weiterverwendung oder weiteren Analyse heruntergeladen werden.","downloadStrange":"Datensatz Strange Internet","downloadRounds":"Rundenübersicht","downloadContention":"Konkurrenz 2012","downloadGlossary":"DNS- & Governance-Glossar","downloadFacts":"Schlüsselfakten","downloadPack":"Gesamtes Datenpaket herunterladen (.zip)","anglesTitle":"Fragen, die sich aus den Daten ergeben","angle1":"Wie die Sprache der TLD-Bewerbungen die Gesellschaft jeder Epoche spiegelt.","angle2":"Von .berlin zu .lugano: wie Städte in die DNS-Root kamen.","angle3":"Warum 'Bewerbung' und 'Delegation' zwei sehr unterschiedliche Momente sind.","angle4":"Was die Konkurrenzsets 2026 über KI, Agenten, Finanzen, Marken und Identität verraten.","angle5":"Kann sich eine Stadt-TLD von digitalem Branding zu Vertrauensinfrastruktur entwickeln?","sourcesTitle":"Quellen & Methodik","sourcesSub":"Primärquellen werden bevorzugt. Sekundärquellen sind ausdrücklich gekennzeichnet.","guardrails":"Methodische Leitplanken","primary":"Primär","secondary":"Sekundär","footerLeft":"Pre-Reveal-Prototyp · Datenstand 1. Oktober 2026","footerRight":"Statisch, local-first und bereit für Webhosting.","applications":"Bewerbungen","applicants":"Bewerber","geographic":"Geografisch","community":"Community","idn":"IDN","round2000":"Proof of Concept","round2004":"Runde für gesponserte TLDs","round2012":"Massive Erweiterung","round2026":"Nächste Runde","tl1984Title":"Vor .com gab es .arpa","tl1984Body":"RFC 920 beschrieb .arpa als anfängliche, für den Übergang gedachte Top-Level-Domain des ARPA-Internet während der Einführung des DNS. Sie sollte verschwinden; stattdessen blieb sie bestehen und dient heute der Internet-Infrastruktur.","tl1985Title":"Die erste .com-Domain: symbolics.com","tl1985Body":"Im Januar 1985 kamen .com, .edu, .gov, .mil, .net und .org zusammen mit .arpa in die Root-Zone. Am 15. März wurde symbolics.com als erste .com-Domain registriert.","tl1999Title":"Die Idee der Stadt-TLD entsteht","tl1999Body":"Das Konzept für .berlin lässt sich bis 1999 zurückverfolgen.","tl2000Title":"Proof of Concept","tl2000Body":"47 Bewerbungen. Sogar .nyc ist dabei, wird später jedoch zurückgezogen.","tl2004Title":"Gesponserte Gemeinschaften","tl2004Body":"10 Bewerbungen für 9 Strings: .asia, .cat, .jobs, .mail, .mobi, .post, .tel, .travel und .xxx.","tl2012Title":"Die Explosion","tl2012Body":"1.930 Bewerbungen, rund 1.400 eindeutige Strings und 66 geografische Bewerbungen.","tl2014Title":"Städte gehen online","tl2014Body":".berlin öffnet für die Öffentlichkeit; .nyc und .paris werden delegiert.","tl2026Title":"Die nächste Ebene","tl2026Body":"1.616 bezahlte Bewerbungen gehen in das Verfahren. Reveal Day: 7. Oktober 2026. .lugano kommt ins Spiel.","city":"Stadt","children":"Kinder","health":"Gesundheit","labour":"Arbeit","publicInterest":"Öffentliches Interesse","techFossil":"Technikfossil","geography":"Geografie","culture":"Kultur","identity":"Identität","publicInfrastructure":"Öffentliche Infrastruktur","oddity":"Kuriosität","socialMirror":"Gesellschaftsspiegel","optimism":"Optimismus","trust":"Vertrauen","adult":"Inhalte für Erwachsene","personalBrand":"Personal Brand","statusCulture":"Status","intimacy":"Intimität","politics":"Politik","faith":"Glaube","lifestyle":"Lifestyle","protest":"Protest","withdrawn":"Zurückgezogen","returned":"Zurückgegeben","evaluated":"Bewertet","selected":"Ausgewählt","proposed":"Vorgeschlagen","delegated":"Delegiert","notDelegated":"Nicht delegiert","longitudinal":"Vorgeschlagen → delegiert","cNyc":"Eine Stadt-TLD-Bewerbung gab es bereits 2000.","cKids":"Mehrere Bewerber wollten einen eigenen Namensraum für Kinder.","cHealth":"Die Weltgesundheitsorganisation schlug einen eigenen .health-Namensraum vor.","cUnion":"Die internationale Gewerkschaftsbewegung bewarb sich um .union.","cWap":"Ein reines Fossil der WAP- und separaten Mobile-Web-Ära.","cGeo":"Ein geografisches Namespace-Konzept lange vor der Stadtwelle von 2012.","cMuseum":"Eine der sieben ausgewählten Proof-of-Concept-TLDs.","cName":"Persönliche Identität direkt im Namensraum gedacht.","cPost":"Öffentliche Infrastruktur; der Postsektor kehrte 2004 mit .post zurück.","sKids":"Im Jahr 2000 schlugen mehrere Bewerber .kids vor; eine Bewerbung kombinierte es sogar mit .xxx.","iKids":"Schon das frühe Web fragte, ob Kinder einen eigenen geregelten Raum brauchen und nicht nur gefilterte Inhalte.","sHealth":"Die WHO schlug einen eigenen .health-Namensraum vor.","iHealth":"Lange vor heutigen Debatten über Gesundheitsdesinformation dachten öffentliche Akteure über Vertrauen bereits auf der Namensebene nach.","sUnion":"Der Internationale Bund Freier Gewerkschaften bewarb sich um .union.","iUnion":"Kollektive Institutionen des Industriezeitalters suchten aktiv nach einem eigenen Ort in der neuen digitalen Öffentlichkeit.","sNyc":"Eine .nyc-Bewerbung existierte bereits 2000 und wurde später zurückgezogen.","iNyc":"Der Wunsch nach städtischer Online-Identität entstand, bevor ICANN einen ausgereiften Rahmen für Stadt-TLDs hatte.","sSucks":"Name.Space schlug .sucks 2000 vor; 2015 wurde eine echte .sucks-gTLD delegiert.","iSucks":"Eine provokante Randidee wurde reale Infrastruktur. Die Grenze dessen, was in die Root 'gehört', hatte sich stark verschoben.","sWar":".war war Teil des riesigen String-Pakets von Name.Space.","iWar":"Der Vorschlag behandelte das DNS fast als Taxonomie des gesamten menschlichen Lebens, einschließlich seiner dunkelsten Kategorien.","sSoup":"Ja: Auch .soup wurde vorgeschlagen.","iSoup":"In der frühen Expansionsdebatte schien fast jede semantische Kategorie eine eigene Top-Level-Domain verdienen zu können.","sMagic":".magic wurde 2000 vorgeschlagen.","iMagic":"Das frühe Internet-Naming trug noch einen utopischen Zug: große Begriffe als eigenständige digitale Orte.","sMobi":".mobi wurde 2004 vorgeschlagen und später delegiert.","iMobi":"Bevor Responsive Design und Smartphones ein einziges Web selbstverständlich machten, schien Mobile verschieden genug für einen eigenen Namensraum.","sMail":".mail wurde als sponsored Namespace vorgeschlagen, aber nicht delegiert.","iMail":"Spam und Vertrauen waren schon damals große genug Probleme, um eine Lösung auf der Namensebene zu versuchen.","sCat":".cat wurde für die katalanische Sprach- und Kulturgemeinschaft geschaffen.","iCat":"Eine TLD musste nicht einem Staat entsprechen: Auch Sprache und Kultur konnten Infrastruktur werden.","sPost":".post kam vom Weltpostverein.","iPost":"Eine der ältesten globalen Kommunikationsinfrastrukturen versuchte, sich in die Architektur des Internets zu übersetzen.","sXxx":".xxx wurde zu einer der umstrittensten sponsored-TLD-Geschichten.","iXxx":"Naming-Entscheide sind nicht nur technisch; sie können Stellvertreter für größere kulturelle und regulatorische Konflikte werden.","sGuru":".guru wurde aus der Runde 2012 delegiert.","iGuru":"In den 2010er-Jahren wurde Expertise selbst zu einer vermarktbaren Online-Identität.","sNinja":".ninja wurde aus der Runde 2012 delegiert.","iNinja":"Die spielerische Sprache der Startup- und Entwicklerkultur erreichte die DNS-Root.","sRich":".rich wurde aus der Runde 2012 delegiert.","iRich":"Status und Exklusivität wurden zu expliziten Identitätskategorien, die online gekauft und signalisiert werden konnten.","sDating":".dating wurde aus der Runde 2012 delegiert.","iDating":"Mit der Spezialisierung der Plattformen wurde auch das intime Leben zu einer kommerziellen Vertikale im Namensraum.","sGop":".gop wurde an eine US-amerikanische politische Organisation delegiert.","iGop":"Auch politische Identität wurde zu einem Marken-Namensraum und zeigt, wie weit die Spezialisierung 2012 ging.","sLgbt":".lgbt wurde aus der Runde 2012 delegiert.","iLgbt":"Gemeinschaft und Identität konnten direkt auf der obersten Ebene des Namenssystems repräsentiert werden.","sBible":".bible wurde aus der Runde 2012 delegiert.","iBible":"Glaubensgemeinschaften reihten sich neben Marken, Orte und Branchen ein und beanspruchten expliziten Raum in der Root.","sBeer":".beer wurde aus der Runde 2012 delegiert.","iBeer":"Produkte waren nicht mehr nur Dinge, die online verkauft wurden; sie wurden zu Lifestyle-Kategorien mit eigenen Namensräumen.","sPizza":".pizza wurde aus der Runde 2012 delegiert.","iPizza":"Alltagskonsum wurde Teil des DNS-Vokabulars, von Branchen und Marken bis zur Esskultur.","sWtf":".wtf wurde aus der Runde 2012 delegiert.","iWtf":"Internet-Slang wurde Infrastruktur: ein knapper Beweis dafür, dass der Namensraum kulturell im Mainstream angekommen war.","sFail":".fail wurde aus der Runde 2012 delegiert.","iFail":"Sogar Scheitern wurde zu einer tragfähigen semantischen Kategorie in der DNS-Root. Das System war weit über institutionelle Namen hinausgewachsen.","pf2026":"Bezahlte Bewerbungen, die ICANN offiziell als im Verfahren bestätigt hat.","pf2012apps":"Bewerbungen in der New-gTLD-Runde 2012.","pfGeo":"Bewerbungen 2012, die in der ICANN-Statistik als geografisch ausgewiesen wurden.","pfYears":"Jahre zwischen der Runde 2012 und der neuen Bewerbungsrunde 2026.","pfBerlin":".berlin wurde 2014 die erste Stadt-TLD, die Registrierungen für die breite Öffentlichkeit öffnete.","plan1":"Offiziellen APS-Datensatz erfassen.","plan2":"Primäre, Ersatz- und Varianten-Strings normalisieren.","plan3":"Konfliktgruppen und Bewerberzahlen aufbauen.","plan4":"Geografie/Städte, Marken, Communities, KI/Agenten, Identität/Vertrauen, Finanzen/Krypto und weitere redaktionelle Themen klassifizieren.","plan5":"Mit 2000, 2004 und 2012 vergleichen.","plan6":"LinkedIn-taugliche Fakten und herunterladbare Datenexporte erzeugen.","guard1":"„Geographic“ ist eine ICANN-Klassifikation für Bewerbungen; „City-TLD“ ist eine engere analytische Kategorie dieser Publikation.","guard2":"Bewerbungszahlen, eindeutige Strings, Registry-Portfolios, als RSP verwaltete TLDs und delegierte TLDs sind unterschiedliche Kennzahlen und werden nicht vermischt.","guard3":"Der Datensatz 2026 stammt aus ICANN APS. Zwischen Reveal Day und String Confirmation Day können sich Strings und Contention Sets durch das Replacement-Verfahren noch ändern.","guard4":"„Zeitgeist“-Themen und das Fünf-Ebenen-Modell der Macht sind analytische Kategorien und bleiben von offiziellen ICANN-Feldern getrennt.","tabBeyond":"Mehr als eine Domain","tabGeo":"Geografie","tabLugano":".lugano","beyondTitle":"Wenn eine TLD zur Infrastruktur wird","beyondSub":"Einige Namensräume gehen weit über einen Namen hinaus: Zugangskriterien, Verifizierung, Sicherheitsanforderungen, gemeinwohlorientierte Governance oder gemeinsame digitale Dienste.","maturityTitle":"Vom Namen zur Vertrauensschicht","maturitySub":"Entscheidend ist nicht die Zahl der Domains, sondern wie viel Bedeutung und Verifizierung der Namensraum tragen kann.","m1":"Name","m1b":"Eine erkennbare Adresse.","m2":"Qualifizierte Gemeinschaft","m2b":"Regeln definieren die Teilnahme.","m3":"Verifizierte Identität","m3b":"Berechtigung wird geprüft.","m4":"Sicherheitsbasis","m4b":"Technische Anforderungen werden Teil des Namensraums.","m5":"Gemeinsame Dienste","m5b":"Die TLD wird zur Plattform für gemeinsame Services.","stageShared":"Gemeinsame Dienste","stageCivic":"Zivile Infrastruktur","stageVerified":"Verifiziertes Vertrauen","stagePublicId":"Verifizierte öffentliche Identität","stageQualification":"Externe Qualifikation","stageCommitment":"Profil + Verpflichtung","stageQualified":"Qualifizierter Namensraum","casePostTitle":"Eine TLD mit Serviceschicht","casePostBody":"Die UPU prüft Registranten, verlangt einen Cybersecurity-Rahmen und betreibt trust.post als Shared Services Platform für den Post- und Logistiksektor.","caseCatTitle":"Eine Domain finanziert ein digitales Gemeingut","caseCatBody":"Das .cat-Register reinvestiert Einnahmen in digitale Rechte, Werkzeuge und Projekte der katalanischsprachigen Gemeinschaft und beschreibt die erweiterte Mission als zivile Infrastruktur.","caseBankTitle":"Sicherheitsregeln als Teil der Zugehörigkeit","caseBankBody":"Aktive .bank- und .insurance-Domains müssen Anforderungen wie DNSSEC, TLS und E-Mail-Authentifizierung erfüllen.","caseGovTitle":"Die Endung wird zum institutionellen Nachweis","caseGovBody":"Nur verifizierte US-Regierungsorganisationen können .gov registrieren. CISA prüft Berechtigung und Identität der Antragsteller.","casePharmacyTitle":"Eine Domain mit Akkreditierung","casePharmacyBody":"Für .pharmacy müssen NABP-Standards erfüllt und eine Akkreditierung erteilt werden.","caseEcoTitle":"Aktivierung mit Profil und Verpflichtung","caseEcoBody":"Eine .eco-Registrierung bleibt gesperrt, bis Berechtigung bestätigt, ein Eco Profile erstellt und eine Umweltverpflichtung abgegeben wurde.","caseSwissTitle":"Ein Namensraum für eine definierte Gemeinschaft","caseSwissBody":".swiss ist der Schweizer Gemeinschaft vorbehalten und verknüpft Registrant und beantragten Namen mit der Schweiz.","sourceCase":"Primärquelle","geoTitle":"Die Geografie der Macht im DNS","geoSub":"Das Internet ist physisch verteilt. Keine einzelne Karte zeigt, wer ‘das Internet kontrolliert’. Regelsetzung, Root-Koordination, Registry-Verträge, technische Kapazität und Nachfrage der Antragsteller konzentrieren sich an unterschiedlichen Orten; diese Ansicht hält die Ebenen getrennt.","geoPlaces":"Repräsentierte Orte","geoGovernance":"Koordination & Root","geoRsp":"Evaluierte RSP-Kapazität · 2026","geoPlacesNote":"Kuratierte Auswahl von Stadt- und Geo-TLDs. Diese Ebene zeigt, was ein Namensraum repräsentiert; sie ist weder ein vollständiger Zensus noch eine Karte des technischen Backends.","geoGovernanceNote":"Ausgewählte Standorte von ICANN, PTI/IANA und Root Zone Maintainer. ICANN-Policy entsteht in einem globalen Multistakeholder-Prozess; PTI validiert Root-Änderungen und Verisign setzt sie um. Standorte zeigen nicht, wo Root-Dienste oder Internetverkehr physisch stattfinden.","geoRspNote":"Bewertete RSP-Organisationen nach ICANN-Region zum 31. August 2026: Europa 16, Asien-Pazifik 7, Nordamerika 6, Afrika 0, Lateinamerika/Karibik 0. Dies ist die Geografie des vorab bewerteten technischen Angebots. Die von Antragstellern gewählten RSPs werden in den öffentlichen 2026-Bewerbungsdaten nicht veröffentlicht (Frage 176); dies ist daher kein Marktanteil.","registryGeoTitle":"Wo „wohnen“ TLDs?","registryGeoBody":"Sinnvoll sind drei Ebenen: repräsentierter Ort, rechtlicher Sitz des Registry Operators und verteiltes technisches Backend. IANA führt den Manager jeder Delegation. Eine vollständige Länderaggregation wird mit dem 2026-Datensatz ergänzt.","powerTitle":"Wo konzentriert sich DNS-Macht?","powerBody":"Policy, Root-Zone-Management, Wartung, Registry-Betrieb und technisches Backend sind verschiedene Rollen. Getrennte Karten zeigen Konzentration, ohne zu behaupten, eine Organisation „besitze das Internet“.","rootStat1":"12 unabhängige Root-Server-Betreiber","rootStat2":"13 logische Root-Server-Identitäten (A–M)","rootStat3":"2.045 operative Root-Server-Instanzen am 30. September 2026","rspStat":"29 evaluierte RSP-Organisationen im August 2026","mapHint":"Ebene auswählen. Marker mit Maus oder Tippen öffnen.","nodeLA":"ICANN-Hauptsitz und Public Technical Identifiers (IANA-Funktionen).","nodeReston":"Verisign-Hauptsitz; Verisign erbringt Root-Zone-Maintainer-Dienste.","nodeRegional":"ICANN-Regionalbüro.","nodeEngagement":"ICANN Engagement Center.","luganoTitle":".lugano — vom Namen zur Vertrauensschicht","luganoSub":"Eine Vision dessen, was der Namespace bei erfolgreicher Bewerbung werden könnte. Eine Richtung, keine Ankündigung bereits realisierter Dienste.","visionBadge":"VISION · NOCH NICHT UMGESETZT","luganoLead":"Die Ambition ist nicht nur, Lugano hinter einen Punkt zu setzen, sondern einen gemeinwohlorientierten Namensraum zu schaffen, in dem Vertrauen sichtbar, steuerbar und später auch maschinenlesbar wird.","lugPillar1":"Öffentliche Verantwortung","lugPillar1b":"Regeln, reservierte Namen und strategische Entscheide bleiben in öffentlicher Verantwortung.","lugPillar2":"Qualifizierte Teilnahme","lugPillar2b":"Unterschiedliche Nutzer- oder Dienstkategorien können klare Berechtigungs- und Verantwortungsregeln erhalten.","lugPillar3":"Sicherheit und Interoperabilität","lugPillar3b":"Technische Mindestanforderungen und offene Schnittstellen können Vertrauen operativ machen.","lugPillar4":"Maschinenlesbares Vertrauen","lugPillar4b":"Langfristig könnten Software und KI-Agenten prüfen, mit wem sie interagieren, was ein Dienst darf und welche Regeln gelten.","lugRoadmap":"Eine mögliche Entwicklung","lugRoadmapSub":"Jeder Schritt würde nach erfolgreichem ICANN-Verfahren eigene Governance-, Rechts- und Technikentscheide erfordern.","lugStage1":"Sichere Registry-Basis","lugStage1b":"Registry-Policies, reservierte Namen, institutionelle Domains, Abuse-Handling und Betriebskontinuität.","lugStage2":"Verifiziertes Ökosystem","lugStage2b":"Qualifizierte Organisationen und Dienste mit klaren Nutzungsbedingungen und transparenten Verantwortlichkeiten.","lugStage3":"Digitale Gemeingut-Schicht","lugStage3b":"Gemeinsame Komponenten, Metadaten und interoperable Dienste für das lokale Ökosystem.","lugStage4":"Agent-ready Trust","lugStage4b":"Maschinenlesbare Identität, Delegation und Service Discovery, damit auch intelligente Systeme Vertrauen bewerten können.","luganoGuard":"Diese Sektion bedeutet weder, dass .lugano bereits zugeteilt wurde, noch dass diese Dienste existieren. Sie beschreibt eine mögliche Richtung, abhängig vom Bewerbungsergebnis und künftigen Entscheiden der Stadt.","geoSourceLine":"Methode: Policy, Root-Koordination, Verträge, technisches Backend und Nachfrage sind getrennte Ebenen. Geografische Konzentration belegt Asymmetrie, nicht dass ein Ort oder Unternehmen „das Internet kontrolliert“.","beyondQuote":"Einige der klarsten Beispiele zeigen: Eine TLD kann mehr tragen als einen Namen — Berechtigung, Identität, Sicherheit, Gemeinschaftsregeln und gemeinsame Dienste.","eyebrow":"ICANN · DNS · STÄDTE · GESELLSCHAFT","fact":"FAKT","reading":"EINORDNUNG","vision":"VISION","legendText":"verifizierte Information · Einordnung der Daten · Zukunftsszenario","delegationRecords":"Delegationsdatensätze · Runde 2012","delegationCaveat":"ICANN meldet 1.241 Delegationen aus der Runde 2012; die Zahl ist nicht um später beendete oder aus der Root entfernte TLDs bereinigt.","source2012":"ICANN-Statistik 2012","sourceReveal":"ICANN Reveal Day","mobileNavigate":"Navigation","roundAtGlance":"Die Runden auf einen Blick","roundAtGlanceSub":"Vier Öffnungen, sehr unterschiedliche Größenordnungen. Angezeigt werden Bewerbungen, nicht die letztlich delegierten TLDs.","applicantRegion2012":"Bewerbernachfrage · 2012","applicantRegion2012Note":"Offizielle ICANN-Regionalverteilung der 1.930 Bewerbungen nach Hauptsitz des Bewerbers. Sie zeigt nicht, wo eine TLD „lebt“.","rspEvaluated":"bewertete RSPs","knownReveal":"Was der Reveal Day zeigen wird","unknownReveal":"Was wir noch nicht wissen werden","knownRevealSub":"Am 7. Oktober um 18:00 UTC veröffentlicht ICANN die öffentlich zugänglichen Bewerbungsdaten.","unknownRevealSub":"Der Reveal startet die Bewertung; er ist keine Zuteilung einer TLD.","known1":"Beantragte primäre Strings und die Bewerber dahinter.","known2":"Gegebenenfalls Variant- oder Replacement-Strings.","known3":"Erste Contention Sets.","known4":"Öffentliche Teile jeder Bewerbung.","unknown1":"Welche Bewerbungen alle Prüfungen bestehen.","unknown2":"Wer in ungelösten Contention Sets erfolgreich sein wird.","unknown3":"Wann eine erfolgreiche TLD delegiert oder gestartet wird.","unknown4":"Dass .lugano bereits zugeteilt sei: Bis zum Abschluss des Verfahrens bleibt es eine Bewerbung.","revealLocal":"Reveal Day: 7. Oktober 2026 · 18:00 UTC · 20:00 Uhr in Lugano","curatedSample":"kuratierte Auswahl","downloadExcel":"Komplette Excel-Arbeitsmappe","excelSub":"XLSX · publizierbare Tabellen + Quellen","dataPackSub":"ZIP · Excel-Arbeitsmappe + alle CSV-Datensätze","footerLeftV05":"Snapshot vor Reveal Day · aktualisiert 2. Oktober 2026 · 1984–2026","footerRightV05":"Primärquellen: ICANN, IANA und zitierte Registry-/Governance-Quellen","roleTagPolicy":"POLICY","roleTagRoot":"ROOT","roleTagGovernance":"GOVERNANCE","roleTagTech":"TECHNIK","roleTagRetail":"REGISTRIERUNG","roleTagUser":"NUTZER","searchLabel":"Strange Internet durchsuchen","roundFilterLabel":"Runde","categoryFilterLabel":"Nach Kategorie filtern","mapLabel":"Kartenebene","methodLegendTitle":"Wie diese Publikation zu lesen ist","methodLegendBody":"FAKT kennzeichnet überprüfbare Informationen mit zitierter Quelle. EINORDNUNG kennzeichnet unsere Interpretation der Daten oder des historischen Befunds. VISION kennzeichnet ein Zukunftsszenario, eine Richtung oder einen Vorschlag. Der Projektstatus wird separat ausgewiesen, weil Status und Aussageart nicht dasselbe sind.","downloadGeoRegions":"Bewerbergeografie 2012","downloadGeoRegionsSub":"CSV · offizielle ICANN-Regionalsummen","beyondDownload":"Jenseits des Punkts","rspDownload":"RSP-Geografie","secondarySource":"SEKUNDÄRQUELLE","statusApplication2026":"BEWERBUNG 2026","statusNotImplemented":"NOCH NICHT UMGESETZT","luganoStatusTitle":"Aktueller Stand","luganoStatusBody":"Die Stadt Lugano hat sich in der ICANN-Runde 2026 um .lugano beworben. Eine Bewerbung ist weder Zuteilung noch Delegation: .lugano wird erst nach den vorgesehenen Prüfungen, dem Vertragsabschluss und der Delegation in die Root Zone aktiv.","operator":"Betreiberquelle","awaitReveal":"OFFIZIELLE DATEN 2026 · EINZUFÜGEN","awaitRevealShort":"Offizielle Daten 2026 einzufügen","afterReveal":"ANALYSE 2026","provisionalUntilConfirmation":"VORLÄUFIG BIS 17. NOV","homeUnique2026":"eindeutige primäre Strings · 2026","homeApplicants2026":"Bewerberorganisationen · 2026","homeContention2026":"erste Contention Sets · 2026","homeBenchmark2012":"Vergleich 2012: 1.930 Bewerbungen · ≈1.400 eindeutige Strings · 230 Exact-Match-Contention-Sets · 66 geografische Bewerbungen.","round2026Title":"Die Runde 2026","round2026Sub":"Vor Reveal Day werden nur bereits bestätigte Zahlen als Fakten angezeigt. Die übrigen 2026-Felder sind für den offiziellen ICANN-Bewerbungsdatensatz reserviert, der am 7. Oktober veröffentlicht wird.","round2026Snapshot":"Vor Reveal Day bestätigte Daten","round2026Unique":"eindeutige primäre Strings","round2026Applicants":"Bewerberorganisationen","round2026Contention":"erste Contention Sets","round2026Geo":"geografische Bewerbungen","round2026Idn":"IDN-/Variant-Bewerbungen","dataStatusTitle":"Reveal Day wird ein Snapshot sein, nicht die endgültige String-Liste","dataStatusSub":"ICANN wird den ersten Datensatz am 7. Oktober veröffentlichen. Berechtigte Bewerber können vom 8. bis 21. Oktober auf einen Replacement String wechseln; die bestätigte String-Liste und aktualisierte Contention Sets sind für den 17. November vorgesehen.","milestoneReveal":"7. OKT · REVEAL DAY","milestoneRevealBody":"Bewerber, primäre Strings, relevante Variant-/Replacement-Strings, erste Contention Sets und öffentliche Bewerbungsdaten.","milestoneReplacement":"8.–21. OKT · REPLACEMENT PERIOD","milestoneReplacementBody":"Berechtigte Bewerber können vom primären auf einen Replacement String wechseln.","milestoneConfirm":"17. NOV · STRING CONFIRMATION","milestoneConfirmBody":"Endgültige Liste der beantragten Strings und aktualisierte Contention Sets.","round2026Patterns":"Was uns die Daten von 2026 erzählen können","round2026PatternsSub":"Derselbe offizielle Datensatz lässt sich aus mehreren Perspektiven lesen, statt ihn auf eine Rangliste zu reduzieren.","patternGeo":"Woher die Nachfrage kommt","patternGeoBody":"Regionen, Länder und Konzentration der Antragstellerorganisationen, abgeleitet aus ihrem öffentlich angegebenen Geschäftssitz.","patternApplicants":"Wer sich bewirbt","patternApplicantsBody":"Rechtliche Antragsteller, Unternehmensgruppen, Wiederholungsbewerber, Marken, Communities, Geografien und Portfolio-Strategien.","patternWords":"Wie sich das neue Internet nennen will","patternWordsBody":"Semantische Cluster, ungewöhnliche Strings und das gesellschaftliche oder technologische Vokabular von 2026.","patternCompetition":"Wo Knappheit bleibt","patternCompetitionBody":"Contention Sets, wiederholte Strings und Bereiche, in denen viele Bewerber dieselbe Idee verfolgen.","geoLayer2026":"Geografische Ebene · 2026","geoApps2026":"geografische Bewerbungen in der Runde 2026","geoApps2026Note":"Nach Reveal Day wird der offizielle Wert aus dem ICANN-APS-Datensatz abgeleitet.","geoCities2026":"Stadt-/Territorial-Strings 2026","geoCities2026Note":"Klassifizierung aus den öffentlichen Bewerbungsdaten 2026.","powerStackTitle":"Macht ist ein Schichtenmodell, kein Punkt auf der Karte","powerStackSub":"Fünf Ebenen sind relevant und beschreiben unterschiedliche Dinge. Sie zu einem einzigen „Zentrum des Internets“ zu verschmelzen wäre elegant, dramatisch und falsch.","powerRules":"Regeln","powerRulesBody":"Der globale Multistakeholder-Prozess von ICANN entwickelt Richtlinien; ICANN Org setzt den vertraglichen und programmatischen Rahmen für gTLDs um.","powerRoot":"Root-Koordination","powerRootBody":"PTI validiert Änderungsanträge für die Root Zone; Verisign setzt validierte Änderungen als Root Zone Maintainer um. Die Root-Bereitstellung selbst ist weltweit auf unabhängige Betreiber verteilt.","powerContracts":"Registry-Verträge","powerContractsBody":"Jeder gTLD-Registry-Betreiber trägt die vertragliche Verantwortung für seinen Namespace. Einige Betreiber halten große Portfolios von Registry Agreements.","powerBackend":"Technisches Backend","powerBackendBody":"Registry Service Provider liefern Registry-, DNS- und DNSSEC-Infrastruktur für viele TLDs. Technische Konzentration kann sich stark von Eigentum oder Registry-Betrieb unterscheiden.","powerDemand":"Nachfrage","powerDemandBody":"Antragsteller entscheiden, welche Strings eine Bewerbung wert sind. Ihre öffentlichen Geschäftssitze zeigen, wo kommerzielle, gesellschaftliche und institutionelle Nachfrage entsteht.","geoMapTitle":"Eine Karte, vier verschiedene Fragen","geoMapSub":"Wechsle die Ebene, um institutionelle Koordination, bewertetes technisches Angebot und Nachfrage der Antragsteller zu sehen. Sie überlappen, sind aber nicht dieselbe Form von Macht.","applicantRegion2026":"Bewerbernachfrage · 2026","applicantRegion2026Note":"Nach Reveal Day wird diese Verteilung aus den öffentlichen Hauptgeschäftssitzen der Bewerber im offiziellen 2026-Datensatz abgeleitet. Sie kartiert Bewerberorganisationen, nicht Nutzer, DNS-Infrastruktur oder die Geografie der späteren Wirtschaftstätigkeit.","demand2012Title":"Bewerbernachfrage · 2012","demand2012Sub":"ICANN verzeichnete 1.930 Bewerbungen aus 60 Ländern und Territorien. Nordamerika und Europa zusammen stellten 82,2 %.","demand2012Concentration":"82,2 % · Nordamerika + Europa","rspSupplyTitle":"Technische Kapazität · evaluierte RSPs","rspSupplySub":"Bis August 2026 hatten 29 Organisationen mindestens eine RSP-Evaluierung bestanden. Europa stellte 16 davon, also 55,2 %.","rspEuropeShare":"55,2 % · Europa","demand2026Title":"Bewerbernachfrage · 2026","demand2026Sub":"Nach Reveal Day wird diese Ansicht aus den öffentlichen Hauptgeschäftssitzen der Bewerber im offiziellen 2026-Datensatz abgeleitet. Sie zeigt Nachfrage, nicht Infrastrukturgeografie.","topCountries2026Title":"Wichtigste Bewerberländer · 2026","topCountries2026Sub":"Nach Reveal Day wird diese Rangliste aus den öffentlichen Hauptgeschäftssitzen der Bewerber im offiziellen 2026-Datensatz abgeleitet.","identityTitle":"Wer baute das breiteste TLD-Portfolio auf? Donuts → Identity Digital","identitySub":"Donuts war 2012 mit 307 über Tochtergesellschaften eingereichten Bewerbungen mit großem Abstand der größte Bewerber. Das Geschäft wuchs anschließend durch Übernahmen und firmiert heute als Identity Digital, das ICANN als weltweit größten TLD-Registry-Betreiber bezeichnet.","identityApps":"von Donuts 2012 eingereichte Bewerbungen","identityPortfolio":"TLDs im heutigen Portfolio von Identity Digital","identityManaged":"TLDs, die Identity Digital nach eigenen Angaben technisch verwaltet","identityHQ":"Hauptsitz · Bellevue, Washington","identityPortfolioClaim":"Identity Digital beschreibt ein Portfolio von nahezu 300 TLDs und gibt zugleich an, als Registry Services Provider technisch 460+ gTLDs, ccTLDs und dotBrands zu verwalten. Das sind unterschiedliche Größen: Portfolio bzw. Registry-Betrieb ist nicht gleich technischer Backend-Betrieb.","identityT2012":"2012 · 307 Bewerbungen","identityT2017":"2017 · Rightside übernommen","identityT2020":"2020 · Afilias übernommen","identityT2022":"2022 · Marke Identity Digital","identityT2026":"2026 · ~300 Portfolio-TLDs / 460+ verwaltet","identityReadingTitle":"Warum entsteht Konzentration?","identityReadingBody":"Registry-Betrieb hat hohe Fixkosten für Infrastruktur, Sicherheit, Compliance und Registrar-Vertrieb. Ein breites Portfolio kann dasselbe Backend und dieselben Vertriebskanäle wiederverwenden, Risiken auf Nischen-Strings verteilen und durch Übernahmen weiter wachsen. Donuts nannte Skaleneffekte ausdrücklich als Kern seiner Strategie 2012.","identityCaveat":"Bewerbungen ≠ erhaltene TLDs ≠ Registry Agreements ≠ TLD-Portfolio ≠ als RSP verwaltete TLDs ≠ Internet-Governance. Die Zahlen gehören zu verschiedenen Ebenen und dürfen nicht addiert werden.","identity2026Top":"Größter rechtlicher Antragsteller · 2026","identity2026TopSub":"Nach Reveal Day wird dieser Wert aus dem offiziellen 2026-Datensatz berechnet. Rechtliche Bewerber werden wie veröffentlicht gezählt; eine separate Ansicht kann Bewerbungen nach öffentlich offengelegter direkter / oberster Muttergesellschaft gruppieren, soweit die Daten dies zulassen.","rootDistributedTitle":"Die Root wird von wenigen Institutionen koordiniert, aber weltweit bereitgestellt","rootDistributedBody":"PTI verwaltet Änderungen der Root Zone und Verisign pflegt die Root-Zone-Datei, während 12 unabhängige Root-Server-Betreiber 13 logische Root-Identitäten über Tausende physische Instanzen weltweit bereitstellen. Institutionelle Koordination und physische Auslieferung haben somit sehr unterschiedliche Geografien.","geoInterpretTitle":"Was „Internet-Macht“ hier bedeutet","geoInterpretBody":"Das Dashboard kann zeigen, wo Policy-Institutionen, Root-Funktionen, Registry-Verträge, Antragstellersitze und technische Anbieter clustern. Es kann Internet-Governance nicht auf Hauptsitze reduzieren und ein großes TLD-Portfolio nicht mit Kontrolle über ICANN-Policy oder die DNS-Root gleichsetzen.","contentionTitle2026":"Wo Bewerber 2026 aufeinandertreffen","contention2026Sub":"Erste Contention Sets werden am Reveal Day verfügbar sein. Sie können sich durch Replacement Strings und spätere String-Confusion-Verfahren noch ändern; der 17. November bietet einen saubereren Vergleichspunkt.","contention2026Label":"erste Contention Sets · 2026","contention2012Title":"Benchmark 2012","contentionCompareTitle":"Wettbewerb ist auch ein kulturelles Signal","contentionCompareBody":"Die am stärksten umkämpften Strings zeigen, wo viele Bewerber unabhängig voneinander denselben Zukunftsmarkt sahen. Der Vergleich 2012–2026 macht aus Contention mehr als ein Verfahrensdetail: einen Indikator veränderter Erwartungen.","downloadRound2026":"Daten Runde 2026 (CSV)","downloadRound2026Sub":"CSV · offizielle 2026-Kennzahlen, Methode und Quellen","downloadConcentration":"Registry-Konzentration","downloadConcentrationSub":"CSV · Donuts / Identity Digital: Chronologie, Kennzahlen und Quellen","downloadGeo2026":"Bewerbergeografie 2026","downloadGeo2026Sub":"CSV · Geografie der Antragsteller aus öffentlichen 2026-Bewerbungsdaten","homeCountries2026":"vertretene Länder und Gebiete · 2026","round2026Countries":"vertretene Länder und Gebiete","round2026Community":"Community-Bewerbungen","round2026Brand":".Brand-Bewerbungen","identityGap":"2012 reichte Donuts 307 Bewerbungen ein, mehr als dreimal so viele wie Google mit 101 als zweitgrößtem Antragsteller; Amazon reichte 78 ein.","identity2026TopGroup":"Größte Unternehmensgruppe · 2026","identity2026GroupSub":"Nach Reveal Day wird die Normalisierung nach Unternehmensgruppen ausschließlich öffentliche Muttergesellschaftsangaben aus ICANN-Bewerbungen verwenden und von der Rangliste der rechtlichen Bewerber getrennt bleiben.","downloadRegions2026":"Geografie Antragsteller 2026 (CSV)","downloadRegistryConcentration":"Registry-Konzentration (CSV)","scaleContrastTitle":"Zwei unterschiedliche Formen von Konzentration","scaleContrastSub":"Die Zahl der TLD-Strings und die Zahl der darunter registrierten Domains ergeben zwei sehr unterschiedliche Bilder von Größe.","identityBreadthLabel":"TLDs im Portfolio von Identity Digital · Breite der Namensräume","verisignDepthLabel":".com- + .net-Registrierungen · Q2 2026","verisignInfraLabel":"Root Zone Maintainer · Betreiber von A-Root und J-Root","scaleContrastReading":"Es gibt keine einzige Rangliste der „Internet-Macht“. Identity Digital bündelt Portfolio-Breite über viele TLDs; Verisign verbindet außergewöhnliche Registrierungstiefe unter .com/.net mit wichtigen operativen Root-Rollen. Keine dieser Größen bedeutet Kontrolle über die ICANN-Politik.","applications2026Download":"Bewerbungsdaten 2026 auf Einzelebene (CSV)","applications2026DownloadSub":"CSV · normalisiertes Schema für den offiziellen öffentlichen Bewerbungsdatensatz 2026","strange2026Pending":"Die Bewerbungsdaten 2026 sind bereits Teil der Publikationsstruktur. Dieser Arbeitsstand wartet auf den offiziellen ICANN-Datensatz; die veröffentlichte Post-Reveal-Version füllt diese Ebene mit realen Strings und Quellen.","tabExplore":"Explorer","exploreTitle":"Namespace Explorer · Vorschau","exploreSub":"Eine kuratierte Suche über ausgewählte aktive TLDs, historische Bewerbungen und den entstehenden 2026-Datensatz. Ein fehlender Eintrag bedeutet nicht, dass der String nicht existiert.","exploreCoverageTitle":"Vorschau-Datenbestand","exploreCoverageBody":"Diese Version indexiert einen kuratierten Seed-Datensatz, während die vollständige IANA-Root und der offizielle Bewerbungsdatensatz 2026 für den Import vorbereitet werden. Die Abdeckung ist bewusst unvollständig und entsprechend gekennzeichnet.","exploreIana":"aktive TLDs im IANA-Root-Snapshot","exploreSeed":"Datensätze in dieser Build","exploreSearchPlaceholder":"TLD, Bewerber, Ort oder Thema suchen…","exploreSearchHint":"Die Suche nutzt Namen und redaktionelle Tags; dadurch können Begriffe wie „Vertrauen“, „Stadt“ oder „Identität“ verwandte Einträge finden.","exploreRound":"Runde","exploreStatus":"Status","exploreType":"Typ","exploreTheme":"Thema","allStatuses":"Alle Status","allTypes":"Alle Typen","allThemes":"Alle Themen","presetAll":"Alle","preset2026":"2026","presetStrange":"Kurios","presetCities":"Städte & Orte","presetContention":"Konflikte","presetTrust":"Vertrauen & Identität","exploreResults":"Einträge","exploreNoResults":"Keine passenden Einträge.","exploreCorpusNote":"Hinweis zur Abdeckung","exploreCorpusNoteBody":"Dies ist eine kuratierte Vorschau, kein vollständiger Index des Namensraums. Nach Reveal Day wird der offizielle 2026-Datensatz importiert und die Abdeckung erweitert, ohne die Oberfläche zu ändern.","exploreColTld":"TLD / String","exploreColStatus":"Status","exploreColRound":"Runde","exploreColEntity":"Bewerber / Betreiber","exploreColGeo":"Geografie","exploreColType":"Typ","exploreColTheme":"Thema","exploreOpen":"Eintrag öffnen","exploreFactFields":"Verifizierte Felder","exploreReadingFields":"Redaktionelle Einordnung","exploreSources":"Quellen","exploreClose":"Schließen","exploreApplicantManager":"Bewerber / Betreiber","exploreGroup":"Unternehmensgruppe","exploreGeography":"Geografie","exploreContention":"Bewerbungen im Konflikt","exploreContext":"Kontext","exploreReading":"Einordnung","typeApplication":"Bewerbung","typeGeneric":"Generisch","typeSponsored":"Gesponsert / beschränkt","typeInfrastructure":"Infrastruktur","typeGeographic":"Geografisch","typeBrand":"Marke","typeCommunity":"Community","typeCountryCode":"Ländercode","legacy":"Legacy","application":"Bewerbung","business":"Wirtschaft","technology":"Technologie","education":"Bildung","government":"Staat","infrastructure":"Infrastruktur","ai":"KI & Agenten","preset":"Ansicht","strangeExplorerCta":"Diese Fälle und den gesamten Namensraum erkunden","strangeExplorerNote":"Strange Internet ist jetzt eine redaktionelle Linse. Suche und Filter liegen im Explorer, wo diese Einträge neben dem restlichen Namensraum stehen.","searchLabelExplorer":"Namensraum durchsuchen","statusFilterLabel":"Nach Status filtern","typeFilterLabel":"Nach Typ filtern","themeFilterLabel":"Nach Thema filtern","explorePlaceholderBadge":"VORLÄUFIGER DATENSATZ","exploreIanaDate":"IANA-Snapshot · 2. Okt. 2026","projectByline":"Unabhängiges Forschungsprojekt von <strong>Robert Bregy</strong>.","projectDisclaimer":"Dieses Projekt gibt keine offizielle Position der Stadt Lugano wieder.","footerAttribution":"Recherche, Analyse und redaktionelle Einordnung: Robert Bregy.","navThemes":"Themen","navRound":"Runde 2026","navResearch":"Recherche","publicationState":"PRE-REVEAL-SNAPSHOT · 2. OKT 2026","publicationStateNote":"Reveal Day · 7. Okt. 2026 · 18:00 UTC","exploreLoadMore":"Mehr laden","exploreShowing":"{shown} von {total} angezeigt","roundAwaitingTitle":"Verfügbar am Reveal Day · 7. Oktober 2026","roundAwaitingBody":"Der offizielle Datensatz wird eindeutige Strings, Bewerberorganisationen, Länder und Gebiete, Contention Sets, geografische/Community/.Brand-Bewerbungen sowie IDN-/Variant-Felder liefern.","luganoVisionCondition":"VISION · FALLS DELEGIERT","shareLabel":"Diese Ansicht teilen","themeLabel":"Darstellung wechseln","languageLabel":"Sprache","closeLabel":"Explorer-Eintrag schließen","drawerLabel":"Explorer-Eintrag","mapItemLabel":"Kartenelement: {name}","resourcesNav":"Ressourcen","methodNav":"Methodik & Quellen","metaDescription":"Von .arpa bis .lugano: wie Internet-Domains zu Identität, Infrastruktur und Vertrauen wurden. Eine unabhängige Forschungsveröffentlichung von Robert Bregy.","metaOgDescription":"Was wir hinter den Punkt setzen, sagt etwas über die Welt aus, die wir uns vorstellen.","tocLabel":"In diesem Abschnitt","homeLabel":"Zur Übersicht","sectionNavLabel":"Abschnitte","explorerPresetsLabel":"Explorer-Schnellfilter","timelineStageProtocol":"PROTOKOLL","timelineStageRoot":"ROOT-MEILENSTEIN","timelineStageIdea":"IDEE","timelineStageRound":"BEWERBUNGSRUNDE","timelineStageLaunch":"DELEGIERUNG / START"},"fr":{"brand":"Marque","copyLink":"Copier le lien","copied":"Copié","heroTitle":"Connecting the Dots","heroBody":"De .arpa à .lugano : comment les domaines Internet sont devenus identité, infrastructure et confiance.","tabOverview":"Vue d’ensemble","tabArchaeology":"Archéologie","tabHow":"Comment ça marche","tabCities":"Villes & territoires","tabContention":"Concurrence","tabStrange":"Internet étrange","tabReveal":"Round 2026","tabPress":"Données & sources","tabSources":"Méthodologie","applicationsRound":"Candidatures par round","applicationsRoundSub":"Le saut de 2012 a changé l’échelle du namespace.","icann2012":"2012 : ce qu’ICANN a reçu","icann2012Sub":"Ce sont des classifications officielles distinctes, pas des parts d’un même gâteau.","overviewCallout":"Ce que nous mettons après le point dit quelque chose du monde que nous imaginons.","overviewFoot":"Les champs officiels ICANN restent séparés de l’interprétation éditoriale. L’objectif est de lire l’histoire sans la transformer discrètement en marketing.","archTitle":"Une courte archéologie de l’expansion","archSub":"Certaines étapes sont des rounds ICANN ; d’autres expliquent comment les city-TLD sont devenus possibles.","timeCapsule":"L’an 2000 ressemble déjà à une capsule temporelle","timeCapsuleSub":"Une sélection du round proof-of-concept.","string":"Chaîne","theme":"Thème","status":"Statut","why":"Pourquoi c’est intéressant","scopeGeneral":"Principes généraux","scopeGeneralSub":"Comment fonctionnent le DNS, les rôles liés aux TLD et le processus des nouveaux gTLD, indépendamment de .lugano.","scopeLugano":".lugano · cas appliqué","scopeLuganoSub":"Ce que ces principes généraux signifient pour la candidature de Lugano au round 2026.","luganoAppliedTitle":"De la règle générale au cas .lugano","luganoAppliedBody":"La candidature .lugano suit le même parcours décrit ci-dessus. Tant que l’évaluation, le contrat et la délégation ne sont pas achevés, .lugano reste une candidature et n’est pas un TLD actif.","luganoExampleLabel":"Exemple hypothétique","luganoExampleBody":"Si .lugano est délégué, la même structure DNS pourra produire des adresses telles que servizi.lugano ou www.servizi.lugano. L’exemple illustre la hiérarchie ; il ne désigne pas un service déjà actif.","luganoLanguageGuide":"Parler de .lugano avec précision","luganoLanguageGuideSub":"Deux distinctions importantes tant que la candidature est encore en cours.","howTitle":"Comment fonctionnent vraiment les domaines Internet","howSub":"Les notions essentielles pour s’orienter entre domaines, DNS et gouvernance d’Internet.","anatomyTitle":"Partons d’une adresse","anatomyBody":"Dans <b>www.example.com</b>, <b>.com</b> est le domaine de premier niveau (TLD). « example » est le nom enregistré directement sous ce domaine. « www » est un libellé optionnel ajouté devant ce nom enregistré.","rootLabel":"Racine","rootBody":"Le niveau supérieur, normalement invisible, de la hiérarchie DNS.","tldLabel":"Domaine de premier niveau","tldBody":"La partie finale : .ch, .com, .org.","secondLabel":"Nom enregistré","secondBody":"Le nom immédiatement à gauche du TLD, par exemple example.com.","hostLabel":"Libellé optionnel","hostBody":"Un libellé optionnel ajouté devant le nom enregistré. Dans www.example.com, « www » peut identifier un site ou un service précis ; il ne fait pas partie du TLD.","whoTitle":"Qui fait quoi ?","whoSub":"Internet n’a pas un propriétaire unique. Il fonctionne grâce à des rôles distincts, des contrats et une coordination technique.","roleIcann":"ICANN","roleIcannBody":"Coordonne les politiques et contrats des domaines génériques de premier niveau selon un modèle multistakeholder mondial.","roleIana":"IANA / PTI","roleIanaBody":"Maintient la base autoritative de la zone racine et coordonne techniquement la délégation des TLD dans le DNS.","roleRegistry":"Opérateur de registre","roleRegistryBody":"Gère les règles et la base autoritative d’un TLD. Le registry operator est responsable de cet espace de noms dans le cadre contractuel d’ICANN.","roleRsp":"Fournisseur de services de registre (RSP)","roleRspBody":"Fournit les services techniques critiques du registre, notamment le DNS et l’infrastructure d’enregistrement.","roleRegistrar":"Bureau d’enregistrement","roleRegistrarBody":"Couche d’intermédiation par laquelle les noms de domaine sont normalement enregistrés et gérés.","roleRegistrant":"Titulaire (registrant)","roleRegistrantBody":"Personne ou organisation qui enregistre et utilise un nom sous le TLD.","birthTitle":"Comment naît un nouveau TLD","birthSub":"En 2026, ce n’est pas “demander et recevoir”, mais un processus public en plusieurs étapes.","step1":"Candidature","step1b":"Le candidat dépose le dossier et paie les frais requis.","step2":"Reveal","step2b":"ICANN publie les données publiques et identifie les concurrences sur des chaînes identiques.","step3":"Contributions & objections","step3b":"Contributions de la communauté, objections formelles et avis gouvernementaux pertinents peuvent intervenir.","step4":"Évaluations","step4b":"La chaîne, le candidat ainsi que les aspects opérationnels, financiers et techniques sont évalués.","step5":"Concurrence","step5b":"Si plusieurs candidats demandent la même chaîne ou des chaînes confusément similaires, la concurrence doit être résolue.","step6":"Contrat","step6b":"Le candidat retenu signe le Base Registry Agreement avec ICANN.","step7":"Délégation","step7b":"Après onboarding et tests techniques, IANA/PTI peut inscrire le TLD dans la racine DNS.","step8":"Lancement","step8b":"Ce n’est qu’ensuite que le registre peut utiliser et attribuer des noms sous le TLD.","controlTitle":"Dans le cas de .lugano : ce que le registre pourrait gouverner et ce que cela ne signifierait pas","canTitle":"Ce que le registre pourrait gouverner","can1":"Critères d’éligibilité et d’attribution des noms sous .lugano, dans le respect des règles ICANN et du droit applicable.","can2":"Noms réservés, phases de lancement et choix de gouvernance locale.","can3":"Exigences d’enregistrement, techniques et de sécurité pour les noms sous .lugano.","cannotTitle":"Ce que cela ne signifierait pas","cannot1":"Lugano ne “possède pas Internet” et ne contrôle ni .ch, ni .com, ni les autres TLD.","cannot2":"Une candidature n’est pas une attribution : .lugano ne devient actif qu’après le processus, le contrat et la délégation.","cannot3":"ICANN n’exploite pas les sites de la Ville ; elle coordonne le système mondial des noms et le cadre contractuel des gTLD.","sourceGuide":"Explication officielle","cityTitle":"Du nom de lieu à l’infrastructure publique","citySub":"L’idée du city-TLD précède le round 2012 de plus d’une décennie.","geoLayer":"Couche géographique 2012","geoApps":"candidatures identifiées comme géographiques","geoDelegated":"gTLD géographiques délégués depuis le round 2012 dans les statistiques ICANN","secondGen":"Une deuxième génération de city-TLD ?","secondGenBody":"La première vague concernait surtout l’identité, la localité et l’enregistrement. En 2026, un namespace urbain peut aussi être conçu autour de services publics de confiance, d’identité vérifiable, de gouvernance lisible par les machines et d’interactions avec des agents IA. C’est une hypothèse éditoriale, pas une classification ICANN.","contentionTitle":"Ce que tout le monde voulait en 2012","contentionSub":"Les concurrences offrent un étonnant instantané de l’ère des apps, des plateformes et du e-commerce.","strangeTitle":"L’Internet étrange","strangeSub":"Pas seulement des noms bizarres. De petits fossiles de ce que chaque époque jugeait digne de son propre morceau d’Internet.","searchPlaceholder":"Chercher .wtf, mobile, identité, confiance…","allRounds":"Tous les rounds","crossRound":"Sur plusieurs rounds","allCategories":"Toutes les catégories","reset":"Réinitialiser","searchHint":"Cherchez un TLD, un thème social ou une idée.","results":"résultats","noResults":"Aucun résultat. Même Internet finit parfois par manquer d’étrangetés.","source":"Source","sourcesN":"Sources","whatItWas":"Ce que c’était","whatItSays":"Ce que cela raconte de l’époque","strangeNext":"<b>Couche 2026 :</b> après le Reveal Day, les candidatures officielles seront classées avec la même discipline que le matériel historique : le dossier de candidature est un FAIT ; le sens culturel ou technologique que nous en déduisons est une LECTURE.","official2026":"État officiel 2026","paidApps":"candidatures payées qui poursuivent le processus","revealBody":"ICANN publiera les données publiques des candidatures le 7 octobre 2026 à 18:00 UTC, notamment les chaînes principales et les concurrences sur chaînes identiques.","preRevealIntel":"Informations pré-Reveal","disclosed":"chaînes rendues publiques volontairement et suivies par TLDz au 26 septembre 2026","secondaryNote":"Source secondaire. Ce n’est pas la liste officielle ICANN et elle sera remplacée par les données du Reveal Day.","importPlan":"Plan d’import du Reveal Day","pressTitle":"Données, faits & ressources réutilisables","pressSub":"Faits vérifiés, définitions et ressources réutilisables pour comprendre les données sans leur faire dire plus qu’elles ne disent.","safeFacts":"Cinq faits clés","copyFact":"Copier","languageGuide":"Des mots précis, des concepts clairs","languageGuideSub":"Les notions essentielles pour s’orienter entre domaines, DNS et gouvernance d’Internet.","sayThis":"Plus précis","notThis":"À ne pas confondre avec","safe1":"La Ville de Lugano a déposé une candidature pour le nouveau domaine de premier niveau .lugano.","avoid1":"Lugano a obtenu / possède le domaine .lugano.","safe2":"Un domaine de premier niveau est la partie finale d’une adresse Internet, comme .ch ou .com.","avoid2":"Un TLD est simplement une nouvelle adresse de site web.","safe3":"ICANN coordonne le système mondial des noms de domaine et le processus des nouveaux gTLD.","avoid3":"ICANN est le gouvernement ou le propriétaire d’Internet.","safe4":"En cas de succès, .lugano pourrait devenir un namespace gouverné selon des règles définies par son registry operator dans le cadre ICANN.","avoid4":"N’importe qui pourra automatiquement enregistrer n’importe quel nom .lugano.","downloadsTitle":"Télécharger les données de travail","downloadsSub":"Téléchargez les jeux de données pour les vérifier, les réutiliser ou les analyser davantage.","downloadStrange":"Dataset Strange Internet","downloadRounds":"Synthèse des rounds","downloadContention":"Concurrence 2012","downloadGlossary":"Glossaire DNS & gouvernance","downloadFacts":"Faits clés","downloadPack":"Télécharger tout le pack de données (.zip)","anglesTitle":"Questions soulevées par les données","angle1":"Comment le langage des candidatures TLD reflète la société de chaque époque.","angle2":"De .berlin à .lugano : comment les villes sont entrées dans la racine DNS.","angle3":"Pourquoi “candidature” et “délégation” sont deux moments très différents.","angle4":"Ce que les concurrences 2026 révèlent sur l’IA, les agents, la finance, les marques et l’identité.","angle5":"Un city-TLD peut-il évoluer du branding numérique vers une infrastructure de confiance ?","sourcesTitle":"Sources & méthodologie","sourcesSub":"Les sources primaires sont privilégiées. Les sources secondaires sont signalées.","guardrails":"Garde-fous méthodologiques","primary":"Primaire","secondary":"Secondaire","footerLeft":"Prototype pré-Reveal · données au 1er octobre 2026","footerRight":"Statique, local-first et prêt pour publication web.","applications":"candidatures","applicants":"candidats","geographic":"Géographique","community":"Communauté","idn":"IDN","round2000":"Preuve de concept","round2004":"Round des TLD sponsorisés","round2012":"Expansion massive","round2026":"Prochain round","tl1984Title":"Avant .com, il y avait .arpa","tl1984Body":"Le RFC 920 décrivait .arpa comme le domaine de premier niveau initial et transitoire de l’ARPA-Internet pendant l’introduction du DNS. Il devait disparaître ; il a survécu et sert aujourd’hui à des fonctions d’infrastructure d’Internet.","tl1985Title":"Le premier .com : symbolics.com","tl1985Body":"En janvier 1985, .com, .edu, .gov, .mil, .net et .org rejoignent .arpa dans la zone racine. Le 15 mars, symbolics.com devient le premier domaine .com enregistré.","tl1999Title":"L’idée du TLD urbain apparaît","tl1999Body":"Le concept .berlin remonte à 1999.","tl2000Title":"Preuve de concept","tl2000Body":"47 candidatures. Même .nyc apparaît, puis est retiré.","tl2004Title":"Communautés sponsorisées","tl2004Body":"10 candidatures pour 9 chaînes : .asia, .cat, .jobs, .mail, .mobi, .post, .tel, .travel et .xxx.","tl2012Title":"L’explosion","tl2012Body":"1 930 candidatures, environ 1 400 chaînes uniques et 66 candidatures géographiques.","tl2014Title":"Les villes passent en ligne","tl2014Body":".berlin ouvre au public ; .nyc et .paris sont délégués.","tl2026Title":"La couche suivante","tl2026Body":"1 616 candidatures payées poursuivent le processus. Reveal Day : 7 octobre 2026. .lugano entre en scène.","city":"Ville","children":"Enfants","health":"Santé","labour":"Travail","publicInterest":"Intérêt public","techFossil":"Fossile technologique","geography":"Géographie","culture":"Culture","identity":"Identité","publicInfrastructure":"Infrastructure publique","oddity":"Curiosité","socialMirror":"Miroir social","optimism":"Optimisme","trust":"Confiance","adult":"Adulte","personalBrand":"Marque personnelle","statusCulture":"Statut","intimacy":"Intimité","politics":"Politique","faith":"Foi","lifestyle":"Lifestyle","protest":"Contestation","withdrawn":"Retirée","returned":"Retournée","evaluated":"Évaluée","selected":"Sélectionnée","proposed":"Proposée","delegated":"Déléguée","notDelegated":"Non délégué","longitudinal":"Proposée → déléguée","cNyc":"Une candidature city-TLD existait déjà en 2000.","cKids":"Plusieurs candidats voulaient un namespace réservé aux enfants.","cHealth":"L’Organisation mondiale de la santé proposait un namespace .health.","cUnion":"Le mouvement syndical international a candidaté pour .union.","cWap":"Un fossile pur de l’époque WAP et du web mobile séparé.","cGeo":"Un concept de namespace géographique bien avant la vague urbaine de 2012.","cMuseum":"L’un des sept TLD sélectionnés dans le proof of concept.","cName":"L’identité personnelle pensée directement dans le namespace.","cPost":"Infrastructure de service public ; le secteur postal reviendra avec .post en 2004.","sKids":"En 2000, plusieurs candidats proposaient .kids ; une candidature l’associait même à .xxx.","iKids":"Le Web naissant se demandait déjà si les enfants avaient besoin d’un espace distinct et gouverné, pas seulement de contenus filtrés.","sHealth":"L’OMS proposait un namespace dédié .health.","iHealth":"Bien avant les débats actuels sur la désinformation sanitaire, des acteurs publics pensaient déjà la confiance au niveau du nommage.","sUnion":"La Confédération internationale des syndicats libres a candidaté pour .union.","iUnion":"Les institutions collectives de l’ère industrielle cherchaient activement une place native dans la nouvelle sphère publique numérique.","sNyc":"Une candidature .nyc existait dès 2000 avant d’être retirée.","iNyc":"Le besoin d’identité urbaine en ligne apparaît avant qu’ICANN dispose d’un cadre mûr pour les city-TLD.","sSucks":"Name.Space proposait .sucks en 2000 ; un véritable gTLD .sucks a finalement été délégué en 2015.","iSucks":"Une idée provocatrice passe de la marge à l’infrastructure réelle. La frontière de ce qui “mérite” la racine s’est fortement élargie.","sWar":".war figurait dans l’immense paquet de chaînes proposé par Name.Space.","iWar":"La proposition traitait presque le DNS comme une taxonomie de toute l’expérience humaine, y compris ses catégories les plus sombres.","sSoup":"Oui : .soup a aussi été proposé.","iSoup":"Dans le premier débat sur l’expansion, presque toute catégorie sémantique pouvait sembler mériter son propre domaine de premier niveau.","sMagic":".magic a été proposé en 2000.","iMagic":"Le nommage du premier Internet gardait une dimension utopique : de grands concepts transformés en lieux numériques autonomes.","sMobi":".mobi a été proposé en 2004 puis délégué.","iMobi":"Avant que le responsive design et les smartphones normalisent un Web unique, le mobile paraissait assez différent pour mériter son propre namespace.","sMail":".mail a été proposé comme namespace sponsored mais n’a pas été délégué.","iMail":"Le spam et la confiance étaient déjà des problèmes assez sérieux pour tenter une réponse au niveau du nommage.","sCat":".cat a été créé pour la communauté linguistique et culturelle catalane.","iCat":"Un TLD n’avait pas besoin de correspondre à un État : langue et culture pouvaient aussi devenir infrastructure.","sPost":".post vient de l’Union postale universelle.","iPost":"L’une des plus anciennes infrastructures mondiales de communication cherchait à se traduire dans l’architecture d’Internet.","sXxx":".xxx est devenu l’une des histoires les plus controversées des sponsored TLD.","iXxx":"Les décisions de nommage ne sont pas seulement techniques ; elles peuvent devenir le terrain de conflits culturels et réglementaires plus larges.","sGuru":".guru a été délégué depuis le round 2012.","iGuru":"Dans les années 2010, l’expertise elle-même devient une identité en ligne commercialisable.","sNinja":".ninja a été délégué depuis le round 2012.","iNinja":"Le vocabulaire ludique des startups et développeurs arrive jusque dans la racine DNS.","sRich":".rich a été délégué depuis le round 2012.","iRich":"Statut et exclusivité deviennent des catégories identitaires explicites à acheter et à signaler en ligne.","sDating":".dating a été délégué depuis le round 2012.","iDating":"Avec la spécialisation des plateformes, la vie intime devient elle aussi une verticale commerciale du namespace.","sGop":".gop a été délégué à une organisation politique américaine.","iGop":"L’identité politique devient elle aussi un namespace de marque, révélant le degré de spécialisation atteint en 2012.","sLgbt":".lgbt a été délégué depuis le round 2012.","iLgbt":"Communauté et identité peuvent être représentées directement au plus haut niveau du système de noms.","sBible":".bible a été délégué depuis le round 2012.","iBible":"Les communautés de foi rejoignent marques, lieux et industries en revendiquant un espace explicite dans la racine.","sBeer":".beer a été délégué depuis le round 2012.","iBeer":"Les produits ne sont plus seulement vendus en ligne ; ils deviennent des catégories de lifestyle avec leurs propres namespaces.","sPizza":".pizza a été délégué depuis le round 2012.","iPizza":"La consommation quotidienne entre dans le vocabulaire DNS, des secteurs et marques jusqu’à la culture alimentaire.","sWtf":".wtf a été délégué depuis le round 2012.","iWtf":"L’argot d’Internet devient infrastructure : une preuve concise que le namespace est devenu pleinement culturel.","sFail":".fail a été délégué depuis le round 2012.","iFail":"Même l’échec devient une catégorie sémantique viable dans la racine DNS. On est loin des seuls noms institutionnels.","pf2026":"Candidatures payées qu’ICANN a officiellement confirmées comme poursuivant le processus 2026.","pf2012apps":"Candidatures reçues lors du round des nouveaux gTLD de 2012.","pfGeo":"Candidatures 2012 identifiées comme géographiques dans les statistiques ICANN.","pfYears":"Années entre le round 2012 et le nouveau round de candidatures 2026.","pfBerlin":".berlin est devenu en 2014 le premier city-TLD à ouvrir les enregistrements au grand public.","plan1":"Capturer le jeu de données APS officiel.","plan2":"Normaliser les chaînes principales, de remplacement et variantes.","plan3":"Construire les ensembles de contention et les décomptes par candidat.","plan4":"Classer la géographie/les villes, les marques, les communautés, l’IA/les agents, l’identité/la confiance, la finance/crypto et les autres thèmes éditoriaux.","plan5":"Comparer avec 2000, 2004 et 2012.","plan6":"Produire des faits prêts pour LinkedIn et des exports de données téléchargeables.","guard1":"« Geographic » est une classification ICANN des candidatures ; « city TLD » est une catégorie analytique plus étroite utilisée dans cette publication.","guard2":"Nombre de candidatures, chaînes uniques, portefeuilles de registry, TLD gérés comme RSP et TLD délégués sont des métriques distinctes et ne sont jamais confondus.","guard3":"Le jeu de données 2026 provient d’ICANN APS. Entre le Reveal Day et le String Confirmation Day, chaînes et contention sets peuvent encore évoluer via le replacement process.","guard4":"Les thèmes « zeitgeist » et le modèle des cinq niveaux de pouvoir sont des classifications analytiques, distinctes des champs officiels ICANN.","tabBeyond":"Au-delà du domaine","tabGeo":"Géographie","tabLugano":".lugano","beyondTitle":"Quand un TLD devient une infrastructure","beyondSub":"Certains espaces de noms sont allés bien au-delà du nom: critères d’accès, vérification, exigences de sécurité, gouvernance d’intérêt public ou services numériques partagés.","maturityTitle":"Du nom à une couche de confiance","maturitySub":"Le changement intéressant n’est pas le nombre de domaines, mais la quantité de sens et de vérification que l’espace de noms peut porter.","m1":"Nom","m1b":"Une adresse reconnaissable.","m2":"Communauté qualifiée","m2b":"Des règles définissent qui peut participer.","m3":"Identité vérifiée","m3b":"L’éligibilité est contrôlée.","m4":"Socle de sécurité","m4b":"Les exigences techniques font partie de l’espace de noms.","m5":"Services partagés","m5b":"Le TLD devient une plateforme de services communs.","stageShared":"Services partagés","stageCivic":"Infrastructure civique","stageVerified":"Confiance vérifiée","stagePublicId":"Identité publique vérifiée","stageQualification":"Qualification externe","stageCommitment":"Profil + engagement","stageQualified":"Espace qualifié","casePostTitle":"Un TLD avec une couche de services","casePostBody":"L’UPU vérifie les titulaires, impose un cadre de cybersécurité et exploite trust.post, une plateforme de services partagés pour le secteur postal et logistique.","caseCatTitle":"Un domaine qui finance un commun numérique","caseCatBody":"Le registre .cat réinvestit ses revenus dans les droits numériques, les outils et les projets de la communauté catalanophone, en qualifiant sa mission élargie d’infrastructure civique.","caseBankTitle":"La sécurité intégrée à l’appartenance","caseBankBody":"Les domaines .bank et .insurance actifs doivent respecter des exigences telles que DNSSEC, TLS et l’authentification des e-mails.","caseGovTitle":"Le suffixe devient une accréditation institutionnelle","caseGovBody":"Seules les organisations gouvernementales américaines vérifiées peuvent enregistrer un .gov. CISA contrôle l’éligibilité et l’identité des demandeurs.","casePharmacyTitle":"Un domaine lié à une accréditation","casePharmacyBody":"Pour obtenir .pharmacy, il faut démontrer la conformité aux normes NABP et obtenir l’accréditation.","caseEcoTitle":"Activation avec profil et engagement","caseEcoBody":"Un .eco reste en attente jusqu’à confirmation de l’éligibilité, création d’un Eco Profile et engagement environnemental public.","caseSwissTitle":"Un espace réservé à une communauté définie","caseSwissBody":".swiss est réservé à la communauté suisse et applique des règles reliant le titulaire et le nom demandé à la Suisse.","sourceCase":"Source primaire","geoTitle":"La géographie du pouvoir dans le DNS","geoSub":"Internet est physiquement distribué. Aucune carte unique ne montre qui ‘contrôle Internet’. Élaboration des règles, coordination de la racine, contrats de registre, capacité technique et demande des candidats se concentrent dans des lieux différents ; cette vue maintient ces niveaux séparés.","geoPlaces":"Territoires représentés","geoGovernance":"Coordination & racine","geoRsp":"Offre RSP évaluée · 2026","geoPlacesNote":"Sélection raisonnée de TLD urbains et géographiques. Cette couche montre ce que représente un espace de noms; ce n’est ni un recensement complet ni la localisation de son backend technique.","geoGovernanceNote":"Localisations sélectionnées de l’ICANN, de PTI/IANA et du Root Zone Maintainer. Les politiques ICANN résultent d’un processus multipartite mondial ; PTI valide les changements de la racine et Verisign les met en œuvre. Les sièges n’indiquent ni où le service racine est physiquement fourni, ni où circule le trafic Internet.","geoRspNote":"Organisations RSP évaluées par région ICANN au 31 août 2026 : Europe 16, Asie-Pacifique 7, Amérique du Nord 6, Afrique 0, Amérique latine et Caraïbes 0. Il s’agit de la géographie de l’offre technique préévaluée. Les RSP choisis par les candidats ne sont pas publiés dans les données publiques des candidatures 2026 (question 176) ; ce n’est donc pas une part de marché des candidats.","registryGeoTitle":"Où les TLD “résident-ils”?","registryGeoBody":"Il faut distinguer le lieu représenté, le domicile juridique du Registry Operator et le backend technique distribué. IANA enregistre le manager de chaque délégation. L’agrégation complète par pays sera ajoutée avec les données 2026.","powerTitle":"Où se concentre le pouvoir du DNS?","powerBody":"Politique, gestion de la root zone, maintenance, registre et backend technique sont des rôles distincts. Les cartographier séparément montre les concentrations sans prétendre qu’un acteur “possède Internet”.","rootStat1":"12 opérateurs indépendants de root servers","rootStat2":"13 identités logiques de root servers (A–M)","rootStat3":"2 045 instances opérationnelles au 30 septembre 2026","rspStat":"29 organisations RSP évaluées en août 2026","mapHint":"Choisissez une couche. Survolez ou touchez un point pour le contexte.","nodeLA":"Siège ICANN et Public Technical Identifiers (fonctions IANA).","nodeReston":"Siège Verisign; Verisign assure la maintenance de la root zone.","nodeRegional":"Bureau régional ICANN.","nodeEngagement":"Centre d’engagement ICANN.","luganoTitle":".lugano — du nom à une couche de confiance","luganoSub":"Une vision de ce que le namespace pourrait devenir si la candidature aboutit. Une direction, pas l’annonce de services déjà réalisés.","visionBadge":"VISION · PAS ENCORE MISE EN ŒUVRE","luganoLead":"L’ambition n’est pas simplement de placer Lugano après un point, mais de créer un espace d’intérêt public où la confiance devient visible, gouvernable et, à terme, lisible par les machines.","lugPillar1":"Responsabilité publique","lugPillar1b":"Règles, noms réservés et choix stratégiques sous responsabilité publique.","lugPillar2":"Participation qualifiée","lugPillar2b":"Des catégories d’utilisateurs ou de services peuvent avoir des règles explicites d’éligibilité et de responsabilité.","lugPillar3":"Sécurité et interopérabilité","lugPillar3b":"Des exigences techniques minimales et des interfaces ouvertes peuvent rendre la confiance opérationnelle.","lugPillar4":"Confiance lisible par les machines","lugPillar4b":"À terme, logiciels et agents IA pourraient vérifier leurs interlocuteurs, leurs droits et les règles applicables.","lugRoadmap":"Une progression illustrative","lugRoadmapSub":"Chaque étape exigerait ses propres décisions de gouvernance, juridiques et techniques après un succès du processus ICANN.","lugStage1":"Fondation sûre du registre","lugStage1b":"Politiques, noms réservés, domaines institutionnels, lutte contre les abus et continuité.","lugStage2":"Écosystème vérifié","lugStage2b":"Organisations et services qualifiés, avec conditions claires et responsabilités transparentes.","lugStage3":"Couche de bien commun numérique","lugStage3b":"Composants partagés, métadonnées et services interopérables pour l’écosystème local.","lugStage4":"Confiance prête pour les agents","lugStage4b":"Identité, délégation et découverte lisibles par les machines afin que les systèmes intelligents puissent aussi évaluer la confiance.","luganoGuard":"Cette section ne signifie ni que .lugano a déjà été attribué ni que ces services existent. Elle décrit une direction possible, soumise au résultat de la candidature et aux décisions futures de la Ville.","geoSourceLine":"Méthode : politique, coordination de la racine, contrats, backend technique et demande sont des couches distinctes. La concentration géographique révèle une asymétrie, elle ne prouve pas qu’un lieu ou une entreprise « contrôle Internet ».","beyondQuote":"Quelques-uns des exemples les plus clairs montrent qu’un TLD peut porter bien plus qu’un nom : éligibilité, identité, sécurité, règles communautaires et services partagés.","eyebrow":"ICANN · DNS · VILLES · SOCIÉTÉ","fact":"FAIT","reading":"LECTURE","vision":"VISION","legendText":"information vérifiée · interprétation des données · scénario futur","delegationRecords":"délégations enregistrées · round 2012","delegationCaveat":"ICANN rapporte 1 241 délégations issues du round 2012; ce total n’est pas corrigé des TLD ensuite résiliés ou retirés de la zone racine.","source2012":"Statistiques ICANN 2012","sourceReveal":"ICANN Reveal Day","mobileNavigate":"Naviguer","roundAtGlance":"Les rounds en un coup d’œil","roundAtGlanceSub":"Quatre ouvertures, des échelles radicalement différentes. Les valeurs représentent les candidatures, pas les TLD finalement délégués.","applicantRegion2012":"Demande des candidats · 2012","applicantRegion2012Note":"Répartition régionale officielle ICANN des 1 930 candidatures selon l’établissement principal du candidat. Elle n’indique pas où un TLD « réside ».","rspEvaluated":"RSP évalués","knownReveal":"Ce que le Reveal Day nous dira","unknownReveal":"Ce que nous ne saurons pas encore","knownRevealSub":"Le 7 octobre à 18:00 UTC, ICANN publiera les données publiques des candidatures.","unknownRevealSub":"Le reveal ouvre l’évaluation; il ne constitue pas l’attribution d’un TLD.","known1":"Les chaînes principales demandées et les candidats correspondants.","known2":"Les éventuelles chaînes variant ou replacement.","known3":"Les premiers contention sets.","known4":"Les parties publiques de chaque candidature.","unknown1":"Quelles candidatures réussiront toutes les évaluations.","unknown2":"Qui l’emportera dans les contention sets non résolus.","unknown3":"Quand un TLD retenu sera délégué ou lancé.","unknown4":"Que .lugano soit déjà attribué: il restera une candidature jusqu’à la fin du processus.","revealLocal":"Reveal Day: 7 octobre 2026 · 18:00 UTC · 20:00 à Lugano","curatedSample":"sélection raisonnée","downloadExcel":"Classeur Excel complet","excelSub":"XLSX · tableaux prêts à publier + sources","dataPackSub":"ZIP · classeur Excel + tous les jeux de données CSV","footerLeftV05":"Snapshot pré-Reveal · mise à jour 2 octobre 2026 · 1984–2026","footerRightV05":"Sources primaires : ICANN, IANA et sources registry / gouvernance citées","roleTagPolicy":"POLICY","roleTagRoot":"ROOT","roleTagGovernance":"GOUVERNANCE","roleTagTech":"TECH","roleTagRetail":"ENREGISTREMENT","roleTagUser":"UTILISATEUR","searchLabel":"Rechercher dans Strange Internet","roundFilterLabel":"Round","categoryFilterLabel":"Filtrer par catégorie","mapLabel":"Couche de carte","methodLegendTitle":"Comment lire cette publication","methodLegendBody":"FAIT signale une information vérifiable appuyée par une source citée. LECTURE signale notre interprétation des données ou du dossier historique. VISION signale un scénario, une orientation ou une proposition future. Le statut du projet est indiqué séparément, car le statut et la nature d’une affirmation ne sont pas la même chose.","downloadGeoRegions":"Géographie des candidats 2012","downloadGeoRegionsSub":"CSV · totaux régionaux officiels ICANN","beyondDownload":"Au-delà du point","rspDownload":"Géographie des RSP","secondarySource":"SOURCE SECONDAIRE","statusApplication2026":"CANDIDATURE 2026","statusNotImplemented":"PAS ENCORE MIS EN ŒUVRE","luganoStatusTitle":"Statut actuel","luganoStatusBody":"La Ville de Lugano a déposé une candidature pour .lugano dans le round ICANN 2026. Une candidature ne vaut ni attribution ni délégation : .lugano ne devient actif qu’après les évaluations prévues, la contractualisation et la délégation dans la zone racine.","operator":"Source opérateur","awaitReveal":"DONNÉES OFFICIELLES 2026 · À INSÉRER","awaitRevealShort":"Données officielles 2026 à insérer","afterReveal":"ANALYSE 2026","provisionalUntilConfirmation":"PROVISOIRE JUSQU’AU 17 NOV.","homeUnique2026":"chaînes primaires uniques · 2026","homeApplicants2026":"organisations candidates · 2026","homeContention2026":"contention sets initiaux · 2026","homeBenchmark2012":"Référence 2012 : 1 930 candidatures · ≈1 400 chaînes uniques · 230 ensembles de contention exacts · 66 candidatures géographiques.","round2026Title":"Le round 2026","round2026Sub":"Avant le Reveal Day, seules les données déjà confirmées sont présentées comme des faits. Les autres champs 2026 sont réservés au jeu officiel de l’ICANN qui sera publié le 7 octobre.","round2026Snapshot":"Données confirmées avant le Reveal","round2026Unique":"chaînes primaires uniques","round2026Applicants":"organisations candidates","round2026Contention":"contention sets initiaux","round2026Geo":"candidatures géographiques","round2026Idn":"candidatures IDN / variant","dataStatusTitle":"Le Reveal Day sera un instantané, pas la liste définitive des chaînes","dataStatusSub":"L’ICANN publiera le jeu initial le 7 octobre. Les candidats éligibles pourront passer à une replacement string du 8 au 21 octobre ; l’ICANN prévoit de publier la liste confirmée et les contention sets mis à jour le 17 novembre.","milestoneReveal":"7 OCT. · REVEAL DAY","milestoneRevealBody":"Candidats, chaînes primaires, éventuels variants/replacements, contention sets initiaux et données publiques des candidatures.","milestoneReplacement":"8–21 OCT. · REPLACEMENT PERIOD","milestoneReplacementBody":"Les candidats éligibles peuvent remplacer leur chaîne primaire par une replacement string.","milestoneConfirm":"17 NOV. · STRING CONFIRMATION","milestoneConfirmBody":"Liste finale des chaînes demandées et contention sets actualisés.","round2026Patterns":"Ce que les données 2026 peuvent nous raconter","round2026PatternsSub":"Le même jeu de données officiel peut être lu sous plusieurs angles, au lieu d’être réduit à un simple classement.","patternGeo":"D’où vient la demande","patternGeoBody":"Régions, pays et concentration des organisations candidates, dérivés de leur adresse professionnelle publique.","patternApplicants":"Qui candidate","patternApplicantsBody":"Candidats juridiques, groupes d’entreprises, candidats multiples, marques, communautés, géographies et stratégies de portefeuille.","patternWords":"Comment le nouvel Internet veut se nommer","patternWordsBody":"Clusters sémantiques, chaînes insolites et vocabulaire social ou technologique de 2026.","patternCompetition":"Où la rareté demeure","patternCompetitionBody":"Contention sets, chaînes répétées et domaines où plusieurs candidats ont convergé vers la même idée.","geoLayer2026":"Couche géographique · 2026","geoApps2026":"candidatures géographiques au round 2026","geoApps2026Note":"Après le Reveal Day, la valeur officielle sera dérivée du jeu ICANN APS.","geoCities2026":"chaînes urbaines / territoriales en 2026","geoCities2026Note":"Classification dérivée des données publiques des candidatures 2026.","powerStackTitle":"Le pouvoir est un empilement de couches, pas un point sur la carte","powerStackSub":"Cinq niveaux comptent et décrivent des réalités différentes. Les réduire à un unique « centre d’Internet » serait simple, spectaculaire et faux.","powerRules":"Règles","powerRulesBody":"Le processus multipartite mondial de l’ICANN élabore les politiques ; ICANN org met en œuvre le cadre contractuel et opérationnel des gTLD.","powerRoot":"Coordination de la racine","powerRootBody":"PTI valide les demandes de modification de la zone racine ; Verisign met en œuvre les changements validés en tant que Root Zone Maintainer. Le service de la racine est, lui, distribué mondialement entre opérateurs indépendants.","powerContracts":"Contrats de registre","powerContractsBody":"Chaque opérateur de registre gTLD porte la responsabilité contractuelle de son espace de noms. Certains opérateurs détiennent de vastes portefeuilles de Registry Agreements.","powerBackend":"Backend technique","powerBackendBody":"Les Registry Service Providers fournissent l’infrastructure de registre, DNS et DNSSEC à de nombreux TLD. La concentration technique peut être très différente de la propriété ou de l’exploitation des espaces de noms.","powerDemand":"Demande","powerDemandBody":"Les candidats décident quelles chaînes méritent une candidature. Leurs adresses professionnelles publiques montrent d’où vient la demande commerciale, civique et institutionnelle.","geoMapTitle":"Une carte, quatre questions différentes","geoMapSub":"Changez de couche pour voir la coordination institutionnelle, l’offre technique évaluée et la demande des candidats. Elles se recoupent, mais ne représentent pas la même forme de pouvoir.","applicantRegion2026":"Demande des candidats · 2026","applicantRegion2026Note":"Après le Reveal Day, cette répartition sera dérivée des établissements principaux publics des candidats dans le jeu officiel 2026. Elle cartographiera les organisations candidates, pas les utilisateurs, l’infrastructure DNS ni la géographie de l’activité économique finale.","demand2012Title":"Demande des candidats · 2012","demand2012Sub":"ICANN a enregistré 1 930 candidatures provenant de 60 pays et territoires. Amérique du Nord et Europe représentaient ensemble 82,2 %.","demand2012Concentration":"82,2 % · Amérique du Nord + Europe","rspSupplyTitle":"Offre technique · RSP évalués","rspSupplySub":"En août 2026, 29 organisations avaient réussi au moins une évaluation RSP. L’Europe en comptait 16, soit 55,2 %.","rspEuropeShare":"55,2 % · Europe","demand2026Title":"Demande des candidats · 2026","demand2026Sub":"Après le Reveal Day, cette vue sera dérivée des établissements principaux publics des candidats dans le jeu officiel 2026. Elle décrira la demande, pas la géographie de l’infrastructure.","topCountries2026Title":"Principaux pays candidats · 2026","topCountries2026Sub":"Après le Reveal Day, ce classement sera dérivé des établissements principaux publics des candidats dans le jeu officiel 2026.","identityTitle":"Qui a constitué le portefeuille de TLD le plus large ? Donuts → Identity Digital","identitySub":"Donuts fut de très loin le principal candidat en 2012, avec 307 candidatures déposées via des filiales. L’activité a ensuite grandi par acquisitions et opère aujourd’hui sous le nom Identity Digital, que l’ICANN décrit comme le plus grand opérateur de registre TLD au monde.","identityApps":"candidatures déposées par Donuts en 2012","identityPortfolio":"TLD dans le portefeuille actuel d’Identity Digital","identityManaged":"TLD qu’Identity Digital déclare gérer techniquement","identityHQ":"siège principal · Bellevue, Washington","identityPortfolioClaim":"Identity Digital décrit un portefeuille de près de 300 TLD. L’entreprise indique aussi gérer techniquement plus de 460 gTLD, ccTLD et dotBrands comme Registry Services Provider. Ce sont des mesures différentes : portefeuille / exploitation d’un registre n’équivaut pas à la gestion technique du backend.","identityT2012":"2012 · 307 candidatures","identityT2017":"2017 · acquisition de Rightside","identityT2020":"2020 · acquisition d’Afilias","identityT2022":"2022 · marque Identity Digital","identityT2026":"2026 · ~300 TLD en portefeuille / 460+ gérés","identityReadingTitle":"Pourquoi la concentration apparaît-elle ?","identityReadingBody":"L’exploitation d’un registre comporte des coûts fixes importants d’infrastructure, de sécurité, de conformité et de distribution via les registrars. Un large portefeuille permet de réutiliser le même backend et les mêmes canaux commerciaux, de répartir le risque entre de nombreuses chaînes de niche et de croître par acquisitions. Donuts citait explicitement les économies d’échelle comme élément central de sa stratégie 2012.","identityCaveat":"Candidatures ≠ TLD obtenus ≠ Registry Agreements ≠ portefeuille TLD ≠ TLD gérés comme RSP ≠ gouvernance d’Internet. Ces chiffres appartiennent à des couches différentes et ne doivent pas être additionnés.","identity2026Top":"Premier candidat juridique · 2026","identity2026TopSub":"Après le Reveal Day, cette donnée sera calculée à partir du jeu officiel 2026. Les candidats juridiques seront comptés tels que publiés ; une vue séparée pourra regrouper les candidatures par société mère directe / ultime publiquement déclarée lorsque les données le permettent.","rootDistributedTitle":"La racine est coordonnée par peu d’institutions, mais servie mondialement","rootDistributedBody":"PTI gère les modifications de la zone racine et Verisign maintient le fichier de la zone racine, tandis que 12 opérateurs indépendants servent 13 identités logiques de la racine via des milliers d’instances physiques dans le monde. Coordination institutionnelle et distribution physique ont donc des géographies très différentes.","geoInterpretTitle":"Ce que signifie ici « pouvoir d’Internet »","geoInterpretBody":"Le tableau de bord peut montrer où se concentrent institutions de politique, fonctions de la racine, contrats de registre, sièges des candidats et fournisseurs techniques. Il ne peut réduire la gouvernance d’Internet à des sièges, ni assimiler un grand portefeuille TLD au contrôle des politiques ICANN ou de la racine DNS.","contentionTitle2026":"Où les candidats se rencontrent en 2026","contention2026Sub":"Les contention sets initiaux seront disponibles au Reveal Day. Ils pourront encore évoluer avec les replacement strings et les procédures ultérieures de string confusion ; le 17 novembre offrira un point de comparaison plus propre.","contention2026Label":"contention sets initiaux · 2026","contention2012Title":"La référence 2012","contentionCompareTitle":"La concurrence est aussi un signal culturel","contentionCompareBody":"Les chaînes les plus disputées montrent où de nombreux candidats ont imaginé indépendamment le même marché futur. Comparer 2012 et 2026 transforme la contention d’un détail procédural en indicateur de l’évolution des attentes.","downloadRound2026":"Données round 2026 (CSV)","downloadRound2026Sub":"CSV · indicateurs officiels 2026, méthode et sources","downloadConcentration":"Concentration des registries","downloadConcentrationSub":"CSV · chronologie Donuts / Identity Digital, indicateurs et sources","downloadGeo2026":"Géographie des candidats 2026","downloadGeo2026Sub":"CSV · géographie des candidats issue des données publiques 2026","homeCountries2026":"pays et territoires représentés · 2026","round2026Countries":"pays et territoires représentés","round2026Community":"candidatures communautaires","round2026Brand":"candidatures .Brand","identityGap":"En 2012, Donuts a déposé 307 candidatures, plus de trois fois les 101 de Google, deuxième candidat ; Amazon en a déposé 78.","identity2026TopGroup":"Premier groupe d’entreprises · 2026","identity2026GroupSub":"Après le Reveal Day, la normalisation par groupe d’entreprises utilisera uniquement les informations publiques sur les sociétés mères dans les candidatures ICANN et restera distincte du classement des candidats juridiques.","downloadRegions2026":"Géographie des candidats 2026 (CSV)","downloadRegistryConcentration":"Concentration des registres (CSV)","scaleContrastTitle":"Deux formes différentes de concentration","scaleContrastSub":"Compter les chaînes TLD et compter les domaines enregistrés sous ces TLD produit deux images très différentes de l’échelle.","identityBreadthLabel":"TLD dans le portefeuille d’Identity Digital · largeur des espaces de noms","verisignDepthLabel":"enregistrements .com + .net · T2 2026","verisignInfraLabel":"Root Zone Maintainer · opérateur de A-root et J-root","scaleContrastReading":"Il n’existe pas de classement unique du « pouvoir d’Internet ». Identity Digital concentre la largeur du portefeuille sur de nombreux TLD ; Verisign combine une profondeur exceptionnelle d’enregistrements sous .com/.net avec d’importants rôles opérationnels sur la racine. Aucun de ces indicateurs n’équivaut au contrôle des politiques de l’ICANN.","applications2026Download":"Données candidature par candidature · 2026 (CSV)","applications2026DownloadSub":"CSV · schéma normalisé pour le jeu public officiel des candidatures 2026","strange2026Pending":"Les données des candidatures 2026 font déjà partie de la structure de publication. Cette version de travail attend le jeu officiel de l’ICANN ; la version post-Reveal alimentera cette couche avec les chaînes et les sources réelles.","tabExplore":"Explorer","exploreTitle":"Namespace Explorer · aperçu","exploreSub":"Une recherche raisonnée parmi une sélection de TLD actifs, les candidatures historiques et le jeu de données 2026 à venir. Une chaîne absente de cet aperçu n’est pas nécessairement inexistante.","exploreCoverageTitle":"Corpus d’aperçu","exploreCoverageBody":"Cette version indexe un corpus initial raisonné pendant la préparation de l’import de l’ensemble de la racine IANA et du jeu officiel des candidatures 2026. La couverture est volontairement incomplète et clairement signalée.","exploreIana":"TLD actifs dans l’instantané de la racine IANA","exploreSeed":"enregistrements indexés dans cette version","exploreSearchPlaceholder":"Rechercher un TLD, un candidat, un lieu ou un thème…","exploreSearchHint":"La recherche utilise les noms et des tags éditoriaux : « confiance », « ville » ou « identité » peuvent donc retrouver des entrées liées.","exploreRound":"Round","exploreStatus":"Statut","exploreType":"Type","exploreTheme":"Thème","allStatuses":"Tous les statuts","allTypes":"Tous les types","allThemes":"Tous les thèmes","presetAll":"Tous","preset2026":"2026","presetStrange":"Étranges","presetCities":"Villes & lieux","presetContention":"En concurrence","presetTrust":"Confiance & identité","exploreResults":"enregistrements","exploreNoResults":"Aucun enregistrement correspondant.","exploreCorpusNote":"Note de couverture","exploreCorpusNoteBody":"Il s’agit d’un aperçu raisonné, pas d’un index complet de l’espace des noms. Après le Reveal Day, le jeu officiel 2026 sera importé et la couverture étendue sans changer l’interface.","exploreColTld":"TLD / chaîne","exploreColStatus":"Statut","exploreColRound":"Round","exploreColEntity":"Candidat / gestionnaire","exploreColGeo":"Géographie","exploreColType":"Type","exploreColTheme":"Thème","exploreOpen":"Ouvrir la fiche","exploreFactFields":"Champs vérifiés","exploreReadingFields":"Lecture éditoriale","exploreSources":"Sources","exploreClose":"Fermer","exploreApplicantManager":"Candidat / gestionnaire","exploreGroup":"Groupe","exploreGeography":"Géographie","exploreContention":"Candidatures en concurrence","exploreContext":"Contexte","exploreReading":"Interprétation","typeApplication":"Candidature","typeGeneric":"Générique","typeSponsored":"Sponsorisé / restreint","typeInfrastructure":"Infrastructure","typeGeographic":"Géographique","typeBrand":"Marque","typeCommunity":"Communauté","typeCountryCode":"Code pays","legacy":"Legacy","application":"Candidature","business":"Économie","technology":"Technologie","education":"Éducation","government":"Gouvernement","infrastructure":"Infrastructure","ai":"IA & agents","preset":"Vue","strangeExplorerCta":"Explorer ces cas et l’ensemble du namespace","strangeExplorerNote":"Internet étrange devient une lecture éditoriale. La recherche et les filtres sont désormais dans l’Explorer, où ces entrées côtoient le reste de l’espace des noms.","searchLabelExplorer":"Rechercher dans l’espace des noms","statusFilterLabel":"Filtrer par statut","typeFilterLabel":"Filtrer par type","themeFilterLabel":"Filtrer par thème","explorePlaceholderBadge":"JEU DE DONNÉES PROVISOIRE","exploreIanaDate":"Instantané IANA · 2 oct. 2026","projectByline":"Projet de recherche indépendant de <strong>Robert Bregy</strong>.","projectDisclaimer":"Ce projet ne représente pas une position officielle de la Ville de Lugano.","footerAttribution":"Recherche, analyse et interprétation éditoriale : Robert Bregy.","navThemes":"Thèmes","navRound":"Round 2026","navResearch":"Recherche","publicationState":"SNAPSHOT PRÉ-REVEAL · 2 OCT 2026","publicationStateNote":"Reveal Day · 7 oct. 2026 · 18:00 UTC","exploreLoadMore":"Afficher plus","exploreShowing":"{shown} sur {total} affichés","roundAwaitingTitle":"Disponible au Reveal Day · 7 octobre 2026","roundAwaitingBody":"Le jeu officiel alimentera les chaînes uniques, organisations candidates, pays et territoires, contention sets, candidatures géographiques/community/.Brand et champs IDN/variant.","luganoVisionCondition":"VISION · EN CAS DE DÉLÉGATION","shareLabel":"Partager cette vue","themeLabel":"Changer de thème","languageLabel":"Langue","closeLabel":"Fermer la fiche Explorer","drawerLabel":"Fiche Explorer","mapItemLabel":"Élément de carte : {name}","resourcesNav":"Ressources","methodNav":"Méthode & sources","metaDescription":"De .arpa à .lugano : comment les domaines Internet sont devenus identité, infrastructure et confiance. Une publication de recherche indépendante de Robert Bregy.","metaOgDescription":"Ce que nous plaçons après le point dit quelque chose du monde que nous imaginons.","tocLabel":"Dans cette section","homeLabel":"Aller à la vue d’ensemble","sectionNavLabel":"Sections","explorerPresetsLabel":"Filtres rapides Explorer","timelineStageProtocol":"PROTOCOLE","timelineStageRoot":"JALON DE LA RACINE","timelineStageIdea":"IDÉE","timelineStageRound":"ROUND DE CANDIDATURE","timelineStageLaunch":"DÉLÉGATION / LANCEMENT"}};
